31
00:01:22,045 --> 00:01:24,790

32
00:01:24,790 --> 00:01:27,130
So a few years ago,
a lot of people

33
00:01:27,130 --> 00:01:30,190
thought that there was
some molten secret sauce

34
00:01:30,190 --> 00:01:32,980
to the song closed
models like GPT-4

35
00:01:32,980 --> 00:01:36,340
and that it will take the open
source community a lot of time

36
00:01:36,340 --> 00:01:38,920
to catch up because the open
source models that we had back

37
00:01:38,920 --> 00:01:42,040
then were much smaller
and less performance.

38
00:01:42,040 --> 00:01:45,100
But now, it seems that
the community figured out

39
00:01:45,100 --> 00:01:49,000
most of the pieces for getting
strong LLMs as it was predicted

40
00:01:49,000 --> 00:01:52,120
in this Google memo that
was leaked and released

41
00:01:52,120 --> 00:01:54,610
on semi analysis.

42
00:01:54,610 --> 00:01:58,690
For example, today we
have Llama 370b instruct,

43
00:01:58,690 --> 00:02:02,320
which has almost the same
performance as GPT-4 but it

44
00:02:02,320 --> 00:02:05,320
unlocked so many use cases
because the model weights are

45
00:02:05,320 --> 00:02:08,800
open, the model can be quantized
and can even run on a consumer

46
00:02:08,800 --> 00:02:09,680
desktop.

47
00:02:09,680 --> 00:02:13,750
It also allows the community to
build very cool use cases on top

48
00:02:13,750 --> 00:02:15,070
through fine tuning.

49
00:02:15,070 --> 00:02:18,340
So we've made a lot of
progress in the open field

50
00:02:18,340 --> 00:02:22,060
and this is not the only
model that's out there.

51
00:02:22,060 --> 00:02:27,030
We're now observing a rise
of open LLMs in the company.

52
00:02:27,030 --> 00:02:30,060
More and more companies are
embracing releasing models.

53
00:02:30,060 --> 00:02:33,320
That was the case, for example,
with DeepMind's Gemma models

54
00:02:33,320 --> 00:02:37,400
and with Mistral's models
and also other models

55
00:02:37,400 --> 00:02:39,860
from GPT [INAUDIBLE].

56
00:02:39,860 --> 00:02:43,760
Here, I put a plot
from the LMSYS Arena,

57
00:02:43,760 --> 00:02:47,930
which is the go to
leaderboard for comparing

58
00:02:47,930 --> 00:02:50,240
abstract models nowadays.

59
00:02:50,240 --> 00:02:52,440
It uses human evaluation.

60
00:02:52,440 --> 00:02:58,740
And you can see in this plot
that as we went from 2023 to May

61
00:02:58,740 --> 00:03:02,570
24, the gap in performance
between the closed models

62
00:03:02,570 --> 00:03:06,080
and the open models is
shrinking and becoming smaller,

63
00:03:06,080 --> 00:03:10,030
which is very promising.

64
00:03:10,030 --> 00:03:13,570
So we're on a very great
path but there are still

65
00:03:13,570 --> 00:03:16,930
a lot of limitations for this.

66
00:03:16,930 --> 00:03:20,860
And this is mainly due
to releases missing out

67
00:03:20,860 --> 00:03:24,460
important details about
how the data was processed

68
00:03:24,460 --> 00:03:26,380
and how the models were trained.

69
00:03:26,380 --> 00:03:29,950
And this is usually the
case for two main reasons.

70
00:03:29,950 --> 00:03:33,040
The first one is to
avoid legal scrutiny,

71
00:03:33,040 --> 00:03:36,370
because when companies
publicly disclose the training

72
00:03:36,370 --> 00:03:39,280
data, if the training
was not done properly

73
00:03:39,280 --> 00:03:42,100
and the copyrighted
were not respected,

74
00:03:42,100 --> 00:03:45,160
they risk facing
legal investigation.

75
00:03:45,160 --> 00:03:49,180
The other reason for not
disclosing the details

76
00:03:49,180 --> 00:03:51,920
can be to maintain
a competitive edge.

77
00:03:51,920 --> 00:03:55,000
So some companies want to
be the best at training LLMs

78
00:03:55,000 --> 00:03:59,140
so they don't want to give all
the details for their training.

79
00:03:59,140 --> 00:04:01,720
Nevertheless, because we
have a lot of releases,

80
00:04:01,720 --> 00:04:04,780
I think we can still answer this
question and put a lot of pieces

81
00:04:04,780 --> 00:04:07,330
together.

82
00:04:07,330 --> 00:04:09,850
So what do we need
to train a good LLM.

83
00:04:09,850 --> 00:04:12,640
The first thing is
probably the model.

84
00:04:12,640 --> 00:04:15,050
You need to have a
good architecture.

85
00:04:15,050 --> 00:04:18,430
And I think now,
transformers are the default,

86
00:04:18,430 --> 00:04:21,160
but there are also other
interesting architectures

87
00:04:21,160 --> 00:04:23,890
like Mamba, which is
a state space model,

88
00:04:23,890 --> 00:04:26,350
or you can use mixture
of experts, which can

89
00:04:26,350 --> 00:04:28,870
be multiple transformer models.

90
00:04:28,870 --> 00:04:31,270
But I'm not going to
spend a lot of time

91
00:04:31,270 --> 00:04:34,090
in this lecture on
models because I think

92
00:04:34,090 --> 00:04:37,400
it's a topic that's
already thoroughly explored

93
00:04:37,400 --> 00:04:40,870
and there are other aspects
that maybe deserve a little bit

94
00:04:40,870 --> 00:04:42,800
more attention.

95
00:04:42,800 --> 00:04:44,390
So that was it for models.

96
00:04:44,390 --> 00:04:46,580
Then for GPUs, I
don't think there's

97
00:04:46,580 --> 00:04:50,690
much I can tell you about that,
except maybe I ask Jensen.

98
00:04:50,690 --> 00:04:54,710
But the part that they
were the most interested of

99
00:04:54,710 --> 00:04:59,540
is the data, which I think
is the backbone of LLMs

100
00:04:59,540 --> 00:05:01,700
because now, almost
everyone is using

101
00:05:01,700 --> 00:05:04,800
the same architecture and
the same training techniques.

102
00:05:04,800 --> 00:05:09,110
And for a given budget, data is
what makes some models better

103
00:05:09,110 --> 00:05:10,080
than the others.

104
00:05:10,080 --> 00:05:13,400
So it's really worth spending
time exploring this data

105
00:05:13,400 --> 00:05:17,120
and understanding how to
get higher quality samples.

106
00:05:17,120 --> 00:05:20,270
So now, we're going to try to
answer our previous question

107
00:05:20,270 --> 00:05:23,705
of how to train a good LLM by
how do we get good training

108
00:05:23,705 --> 00:05:24,205
data?

109
00:05:24,205 --> 00:05:27,420

110
00:05:27,420 --> 00:05:30,430
And I think the answer
to this is threefold.

111
00:05:30,430 --> 00:05:33,720
First, we need to understand
how much data do we need?

112
00:05:33,720 --> 00:05:36,690
And then once we figured
out the size of the data

113
00:05:36,690 --> 00:05:40,600
that we need, where can we
get this data and to clean it,

114
00:05:40,600 --> 00:05:42,840
which filtering
techniques make more sense

115
00:05:42,840 --> 00:05:45,720
and will give us the
best performance.

116
00:05:45,720 --> 00:05:50,130
So to answer the first
one, the answer to that

117
00:05:50,130 --> 00:05:51,660
is the scaling laws.

118
00:05:51,660 --> 00:05:54,330
You want to know which
data, how much data you

119
00:05:54,330 --> 00:05:56,670
want to train your model
on, but also what is

120
00:05:56,670 --> 00:05:58,230
the optimal size of the model.

121
00:05:58,230 --> 00:06:01,710
And the scaling
laws try to study

122
00:06:01,710 --> 00:06:04,530
the allocation of a
compute budget between data

123
00:06:04,530 --> 00:06:06,010
size and model size.

124
00:06:06,010 --> 00:06:08,370
This means should you
take a smaller model

125
00:06:08,370 --> 00:06:10,740
and train it longer
or take a larger model

126
00:06:10,740 --> 00:06:13,110
and train it on less data.

127
00:06:13,110 --> 00:06:19,200
And I'm going to present a brief
history of the scaling laws

128
00:06:19,200 --> 00:06:21,750
because I think it's really
interesting to see how

129
00:06:21,750 --> 00:06:25,290
the sizes of the models
progress through time and also

130
00:06:25,290 --> 00:06:28,360
how the size of the data sets
and the number of tokens we

131
00:06:28,360 --> 00:06:30,880
train on them have changed
because there were really

132
00:06:30,880 --> 00:06:33,640
some drastic changes in that.

133
00:06:33,640 --> 00:06:37,510
I think the first to
establish the scaling

134
00:06:37,510 --> 00:06:40,750
laws were Kaplan from OpenAI.

135
00:06:40,750 --> 00:06:45,670
And they tried to fit the
law as a function of--

136
00:06:45,670 --> 00:06:49,510
the laws as a function of
the data size and model size.

137
00:06:49,510 --> 00:06:52,390
And they found that
if you have a 10 times

138
00:06:52,390 --> 00:06:56,170
increase in your compute, you
should increase your parameter

139
00:06:56,170 --> 00:06:59,170
count by 5.5, but
your training tokens,

140
00:06:59,170 --> 00:07:01,900
you should only
increase them by 1.8.

141
00:07:01,900 --> 00:07:05,260
This means that if you have more
resources to train your models,

142
00:07:05,260 --> 00:07:08,330
you should make the model much
larger but the data is fine.

143
00:07:08,330 --> 00:07:10,990
You shouldn't
increase it that much.

144
00:07:10,990 --> 00:07:14,410
And this is what led to
models like GPT-3, which

145
00:07:14,410 --> 00:07:17,770
is 175 billion
parameters, but it

146
00:07:17,770 --> 00:07:21,430
was only trained on $300 billion
tokens, which, if we think

147
00:07:21,430 --> 00:07:24,400
about it now, is really small.

148
00:07:24,400 --> 00:07:26,860
Other models also followed
this, for example,

149
00:07:26,860 --> 00:07:29,650
like OPT, which was
the same size as GPT-3

150
00:07:29,650 --> 00:07:31,820
and trained on a
similar amount of data.

151
00:07:31,820 --> 00:07:34,150
There was also BLOOM.

152
00:07:34,150 --> 00:07:38,020
So all these models are
actually very under trained.

153
00:07:38,020 --> 00:07:41,020
Then the Chinchilla
scaling laws came after

154
00:07:41,020 --> 00:07:44,440
and they revisited
the scaling laws.

155
00:07:44,440 --> 00:07:47,680
And they found that
the reason Kaplan

156
00:07:47,680 --> 00:07:50,590
thought that data
should not be as scaled

157
00:07:50,590 --> 00:07:54,730
as model size is because they
used a fixed cosine scheduler

158
00:07:54,730 --> 00:07:56,300
for all their experiments.

159
00:07:56,300 --> 00:07:58,510
So although they were
changing the data size,

160
00:07:58,510 --> 00:08:00,740
the cosine scheduler was fixed.

161
00:08:00,740 --> 00:08:02,920
This meant that for
some models, they

162
00:08:02,920 --> 00:08:05,650
were underestimated
because they were not

163
00:08:05,650 --> 00:08:09,970
using the correct cosine that
corresponded to the data size.

164
00:08:09,970 --> 00:08:13,810
This led to false conclusions
and the Chinchilla

165
00:08:13,810 --> 00:08:16,810
can now give us new
scaling laws that

166
00:08:16,810 --> 00:08:19,030
say that you should
scale your data

167
00:08:19,030 --> 00:08:21,430
and your model size equally.

168
00:08:21,430 --> 00:08:25,390
And in their paper,
they train a 65 billion

169
00:08:25,390 --> 00:08:29,920
model on 1.6 trillion tokens,
which is the Chinchilla optimal

170
00:08:29,920 --> 00:08:30,590
point.

171
00:08:30,590 --> 00:08:34,900
And they outperform much larger
models like GPT-3 and Gopher,

172
00:08:34,900 --> 00:08:39,909
which was over 200
billion parameters.

173
00:08:39,909 --> 00:08:42,309
So here, for example,
I have a plot

174
00:08:42,309 --> 00:08:44,390
which shows what the
scaling laws try to do.

175
00:08:44,390 --> 00:08:47,470
For example, here you
have IsoFLOP curves, which

176
00:08:47,470 --> 00:08:49,390
each curve uses a fixed budget.

177
00:08:49,390 --> 00:08:52,000
And then you try to find
the sweet spot, which

178
00:08:52,000 --> 00:08:54,730
is the optimal for
your budget allocation

179
00:08:54,730 --> 00:08:56,890
and it tells you what
your model size should be

180
00:08:56,890 --> 00:09:00,610
and what your data
size should be.

181
00:09:00,610 --> 00:09:03,880
And as you can see here,
if we try to fit the loss,

182
00:09:03,880 --> 00:09:07,900
we can see that there's a linear
increase for data and also model

183
00:09:07,900 --> 00:09:08,400
size.

184
00:09:08,400 --> 00:09:11,260

185
00:09:11,260 --> 00:09:15,190
In this scheme, I
tried to show how

186
00:09:15,190 --> 00:09:19,840
we've moved from the Chinchilla
scaling laws to today's models.

187
00:09:19,840 --> 00:09:22,870
And you can see that, for
example, the Chinchilla model,

188
00:09:22,870 --> 00:09:25,060
which is 60 billion
parameters, was trained

189
00:09:25,060 --> 00:09:26,920
on less than 2 trillion tokens.

190
00:09:26,920 --> 00:09:29,230
But then after
that, we have Llama,

191
00:09:29,230 --> 00:09:32,470
which was released last year,
and it was just a 7B model

192
00:09:32,470 --> 00:09:36,400
and it was trained on as much
data as the Chinchilla model.

193
00:09:36,400 --> 00:09:40,570
So it was trained way past
the Chinchilla optimal point.

194
00:09:40,570 --> 00:09:44,920
And we might be wondering,
why is that the case?

195
00:09:44,920 --> 00:09:49,810
Did Meta not use their compute
budgets in an optimal way?

196
00:09:49,810 --> 00:09:53,620
And the answer to that is that
compute optimal is not always

197
00:09:53,620 --> 00:09:56,500
optimal because when
you train a model,

198
00:09:56,500 --> 00:09:58,750
you don't only care
about what you're

199
00:09:58,750 --> 00:10:02,630
going to spend in training, but
you also care about inference.

200
00:10:02,630 --> 00:10:04,280
And the model is
trained one time

201
00:10:04,280 --> 00:10:07,070
but inference is for more the
model is going to be served.

202
00:10:07,070 --> 00:10:09,110
So you want to save
some cost in that.

203
00:10:09,110 --> 00:10:12,980
This makes it that people prefer
training smaller models longer

204
00:10:12,980 --> 00:10:15,140
than actually using
much larger models that

205
00:10:15,140 --> 00:10:16,480
are trained on less data.

206
00:10:16,480 --> 00:10:18,380
So this was the
case for Llama 1,

207
00:10:18,380 --> 00:10:21,470
for other models like
Mistral, but also for Llama 3

208
00:10:21,470 --> 00:10:25,070
which went even further and
trained not on 1 trillion tokens

209
00:10:25,070 --> 00:10:27,230
but on 15 trillion tokens.

210
00:10:27,230 --> 00:10:29,570
And if you check
the archive paper,

211
00:10:29,570 --> 00:10:34,250
the loss kept going down and
also the downstream evaluations

212
00:10:34,250 --> 00:10:37,963
as the model training
is kept improving.

213
00:10:37,963 --> 00:10:39,380
I think this is
really interesting

214
00:10:39,380 --> 00:10:41,810
because some people
misunderstood the Chinchilla

215
00:10:41,810 --> 00:10:44,370
scaling laws as compute
optimal, optimal

216
00:10:44,370 --> 00:10:48,200
but that's not the case because
the inference cost is not

217
00:10:48,200 --> 00:10:48,930
considered.

218
00:10:48,930 --> 00:10:53,240
So for example, this is the
cost for training GPT-4.

219
00:10:53,240 --> 00:10:57,680
It is said that it's estimated
that $100 million, but also

220
00:10:57,680 --> 00:11:00,230
the inference is very
expensive and the larger

221
00:11:00,230 --> 00:11:05,210
the model becomes, the more
time it takes to process tokens.

222
00:11:05,210 --> 00:11:08,540
So inference, the scaling laws
don't take the inference costs

223
00:11:08,540 --> 00:11:09,800
into consideration.

224
00:11:09,800 --> 00:11:12,110
And if we do take the
inference cost, which

225
00:11:12,110 --> 00:11:15,950
is the case for most
people because they want

226
00:11:15,950 --> 00:11:17,600
to use these models
in inference,

227
00:11:17,600 --> 00:11:21,620
you might prefer using smaller
models and training them longer.

228
00:11:21,620 --> 00:11:24,380
And we do that, we're not
respecting the Chinchilla

229
00:11:24,380 --> 00:11:26,900
scaling laws, so we're
choosing to pay what

230
00:11:26,900 --> 00:11:28,980
we call a compute overhead.

231
00:11:28,980 --> 00:11:32,130
It's a sacrifice that you
do during the training.

232
00:11:32,130 --> 00:11:34,915
You choose to pay more, but
this will have a benefit

233
00:11:34,915 --> 00:11:36,290
during inference
because you will

234
00:11:36,290 --> 00:11:39,900
save a lot of cost and money.

235
00:11:39,900 --> 00:11:44,070
And there's this very
interesting blog post

236
00:11:44,070 --> 00:11:49,740
about Hartman's law, which tries
to measure the compute overhead

237
00:11:49,740 --> 00:11:53,340
that you will be paying when you
choose to train a small model.

238
00:11:53,340 --> 00:11:55,440
For example, here, there's
the space on Hugging

239
00:11:55,440 --> 00:11:57,960
Face where you can
input the model size

240
00:11:57,960 --> 00:11:59,730
and what data sets
you want to train on,

241
00:11:59,730 --> 00:12:04,020
and it will show you where you
are regarding the Chinchilla

242
00:12:04,020 --> 00:12:05,000
optimal point.

243
00:12:05,000 --> 00:12:07,800
So, for example, if we take
7B model and we train it on 1

244
00:12:07,800 --> 00:12:10,810
billion tokens, you can
see that we are here.

245
00:12:10,810 --> 00:12:14,190
It's the red dots and it's
before the Chinchilla optimal

246
00:12:14,190 --> 00:12:14,980
model.

247
00:12:14,980 --> 00:12:20,480
And this gives approximately,
I think, a 40% overhead.

248
00:12:20,480 --> 00:12:23,850
But then during inference, as
it shows here in the table--

249
00:12:23,850 --> 00:12:28,980
sorry, it was 13% overhead, but
there's almost 50% saving costs.

250
00:12:28,980 --> 00:12:31,710
So that's something that's
almost everyone is doing now,

251
00:12:31,710 --> 00:12:34,590
which is why we see models
that are much, much smaller

252
00:12:34,590 --> 00:12:36,215
than one or two years ago.

253
00:12:36,215 --> 00:12:39,550

254
00:12:39,550 --> 00:12:42,610
For further reading, there are
some very interesting papers

255
00:12:42,610 --> 00:12:43,940
about scaling laws.

256
00:12:43,940 --> 00:12:47,380
For example, this paper called
Scaling Data-Constrained

257
00:12:47,380 --> 00:12:51,850
Language Models, which shows
that if you are limited

258
00:12:51,850 --> 00:12:54,010
in your data size,
let's say, for example,

259
00:12:54,010 --> 00:12:57,940
you wanted to finish 7B on
10 trillion tokens but you

260
00:12:57,940 --> 00:12:59,730
don't have these
10 trillion tokens,

261
00:12:59,730 --> 00:13:03,100
this paper says that you can
basically repeat your data up

262
00:13:03,100 --> 00:13:06,190
to four times, so four epochs,
and you will get similar

263
00:13:06,190 --> 00:13:08,840
performance as if you
use the unique tokens.

264
00:13:08,840 --> 00:13:12,790
So for example, instead of
using a trillion tokens unique,

265
00:13:12,790 --> 00:13:15,190
you could use just 2 and
repeat them four times

266
00:13:15,190 --> 00:13:17,380
and you get almost
the same performance

267
00:13:17,380 --> 00:13:20,110
as if these tokens were unique.

268
00:13:20,110 --> 00:13:22,990
And this is especially
useful for some domains

269
00:13:22,990 --> 00:13:28,000
where we almost exhaust all the
data that's publicly available.

270
00:13:28,000 --> 00:13:30,430
As I will show you later,
the Stack v2, which

271
00:13:30,430 --> 00:13:32,980
is a code data set
that we released,

272
00:13:32,980 --> 00:13:36,740
I think it has almost all the
code that is available publicly

273
00:13:36,740 --> 00:13:40,740
so it's going to be very hard
to scrape and get more code.

274
00:13:40,740 --> 00:13:42,800
And if you want to
train models longer,

275
00:13:42,800 --> 00:13:46,410
the only option is to actually
repeat the data during training.

276
00:13:46,410 --> 00:13:49,670
And this is good news
because repeating the data up

277
00:13:49,670 --> 00:13:53,720
to four times is
actually significant.

278
00:13:53,720 --> 00:13:56,090
Another paper that I
think is interesting

279
00:13:56,090 --> 00:14:01,370
when it comes to scaling
laws is the DeepSeek LLM.

280
00:14:01,370 --> 00:14:05,000
They try to establish
new scaling laws that

281
00:14:05,000 --> 00:14:09,410
are suited for the data because
they found that the scaling

282
00:14:09,410 --> 00:14:13,170
behavior is highly dependent
on the data quality.

283
00:14:13,170 --> 00:14:16,400
So they tried different data
subsets, different filterings,

284
00:14:16,400 --> 00:14:19,080
and they found that the
scaling laws were changing.

285
00:14:19,080 --> 00:14:21,870
So this is very important
because up until now,

286
00:14:21,870 --> 00:14:23,930
we were using the Chinchilla,
but the Chinchilla

287
00:14:23,930 --> 00:14:25,580
was using fixed data sets.

288
00:14:25,580 --> 00:14:28,550
They are not necessarily the
ones that we are using now,

289
00:14:28,550 --> 00:14:31,260
so it's really important
to be aware of that.

290
00:14:31,260 --> 00:14:33,530
And this is why DeepSeek
tried to come up

291
00:14:33,530 --> 00:14:36,660
with their own scaling laws
that work for their data sets.

292
00:14:36,660 --> 00:14:40,280
And they also conclude that
when you have higher quality

293
00:14:40,280 --> 00:14:44,360
data sets, maybe more compute
should be allocated to the model

294
00:14:44,360 --> 00:14:46,770
size and not to the data size.

295
00:14:46,770 --> 00:14:49,760
So these are interesting
things to keep in mind

296
00:14:49,760 --> 00:14:51,670
when it comes to scaling LLMs.

297
00:14:51,670 --> 00:14:54,780

298
00:14:54,780 --> 00:14:58,200
So we have answered the
first question, I hope,

299
00:14:58,200 --> 00:15:01,290
how much data to train LLMs.

300
00:15:01,290 --> 00:15:05,010
So let's say now you have
your compute budget, a fixed

301
00:15:05,010 --> 00:15:07,890
number of GPUs for a
certain amount of days,

302
00:15:07,890 --> 00:15:12,720
and you also know approximately
how much data you want to use.

303
00:15:12,720 --> 00:15:17,220
The question is that, where
do you find this type of data?

304
00:15:17,220 --> 00:15:20,280
For example, Llama 3 was
trained on 15 trillion tokens,

305
00:15:20,280 --> 00:15:22,660
but where do you get
15 trillion tokens?

306
00:15:22,660 --> 00:15:26,250
That's a huge number.

307
00:15:26,250 --> 00:15:29,700
To get this data,
the two main sources

308
00:15:29,700 --> 00:15:33,240
where you can actually get
very large volume of data

309
00:15:33,240 --> 00:15:36,720
are the web and
then GitHub code.

310
00:15:36,720 --> 00:15:40,080
There are some other curated
sources that are of high quality

311
00:15:40,080 --> 00:15:43,860
but are much smaller, like
Wikipedia books, archive,

312
00:15:43,860 --> 00:15:45,240
or StackExchange.

313
00:15:45,240 --> 00:15:48,570
You can also get
data, a new type

314
00:15:48,570 --> 00:15:51,210
that's been very
trendy recently,

315
00:15:51,210 --> 00:15:53,570
which is synthetic data.

316
00:15:53,570 --> 00:15:56,680
But let's first start
with the sources where

317
00:15:56,680 --> 00:15:59,880
you can get very large volumes.

318
00:15:59,880 --> 00:16:02,130
The first one is web data.

319
00:16:02,130 --> 00:16:05,580
So that's basically web pages.

320
00:16:05,580 --> 00:16:08,280
And usually, people
to create these data

321
00:16:08,280 --> 00:16:10,350
sets, they start
from CommonCrawl,

322
00:16:10,350 --> 00:16:15,030
which is a public repository
of crawled web pages.

323
00:16:15,030 --> 00:16:17,310
CommonCrawl crawls
pages regularly

324
00:16:17,310 --> 00:16:20,928
and they publish dumps
every few months.

325
00:16:20,928 --> 00:16:22,470
But if you start
from there, you will

326
00:16:22,470 --> 00:16:26,050
need to do some heavy filtering
at a very large scale.

327
00:16:26,050 --> 00:16:29,700
For example, just the latest
dump has over 400 terabytes,

328
00:16:29,700 --> 00:16:32,730
and they have almost 95 dumps.

329
00:16:32,730 --> 00:16:37,140
So that's not a very
easy task, and you

330
00:16:37,140 --> 00:16:40,350
will need to have a lot
of resources and a team

331
00:16:40,350 --> 00:16:43,050
to be able to do that crawling.

332
00:16:43,050 --> 00:16:47,280
The other option is to use
an existing filtered web

333
00:16:47,280 --> 00:16:51,120
data set where researchers
already filtered the CommonCrawl

334
00:16:51,120 --> 00:16:52,360
and released them.

335
00:16:52,360 --> 00:16:54,240
And luckily, we
do have data sets

336
00:16:54,240 --> 00:16:56,850
that are very large
and well filtered.

337
00:16:56,850 --> 00:17:01,510
One of them is the web data
FineWeb, that was recently

338
00:17:01,510 --> 00:17:03,730
released by Hugging
Face, and it has

339
00:17:03,730 --> 00:17:07,199
15 trillion tokens of web data.

340
00:17:07,199 --> 00:17:10,440

341
00:17:10,440 --> 00:17:12,930
It's not just a large
data set, but it also

342
00:17:12,930 --> 00:17:16,290
has the best performance among
the publicly available data

343
00:17:16,290 --> 00:17:17,140
sets.

344
00:17:17,140 --> 00:17:20,400
And here, for example,
it shows the performance,

345
00:17:20,400 --> 00:17:24,420
which is an aggregation over
multiple popular benchmarks

346
00:17:24,420 --> 00:17:29,170
for NLP like HellaSwag,
MMLU, and others.

347
00:17:29,170 --> 00:17:32,070
And it's averages them and
compares to other data sets

348
00:17:32,070 --> 00:17:35,410
like C4, RefinedWeb,
SlimPajama, and The Pile.

349
00:17:35,410 --> 00:17:38,170

350
00:17:38,170 --> 00:17:39,500
So that was for web.

351
00:17:39,500 --> 00:17:42,440
So you can get 15
trillion tokens easily.

352
00:17:42,440 --> 00:17:46,960
And then for code data, we
have released the stack data

353
00:17:46,960 --> 00:17:52,330
set, which is the largest
data set of open source code.

354
00:17:52,330 --> 00:17:54,590
This data set comes
in two versions.

355
00:17:54,590 --> 00:17:58,760
Version one consisted of six
terabytes of permissive code.

356
00:17:58,760 --> 00:18:01,360
And how we build
this data set is

357
00:18:01,360 --> 00:18:05,540
that we first cloned all the
public repositories on GitHub.

358
00:18:05,540 --> 00:18:09,280
So this gave us over
130 repositories

359
00:18:09,280 --> 00:18:11,440
and 100 terabytes of data.

360
00:18:11,440 --> 00:18:14,320
But we don't want all of
that data because a lot of it

361
00:18:14,320 --> 00:18:17,590
can be configs or extensions
that we don't need

362
00:18:17,590 --> 00:18:19,810
or languages that are
no longer maintained.

363
00:18:19,810 --> 00:18:23,290
So we did some file
extension filtering

364
00:18:23,290 --> 00:18:27,190
and we ended up with almost
90 terabytes of data.

365
00:18:27,190 --> 00:18:29,410
After that, we
filtered repositories

366
00:18:29,410 --> 00:18:33,460
based on their licenses so we
can have permissive licenses

367
00:18:33,460 --> 00:18:35,620
like Apache 2 or MIT.

368
00:18:35,620 --> 00:18:38,800
You can have more restrictive
licenses like GPL.

369
00:18:38,800 --> 00:18:41,780
So we filtered all the
repositories that did not

370
00:18:41,780 --> 00:18:43,910
have a permissive license.

371
00:18:43,910 --> 00:18:46,340
And after that, we
did a deduplication

372
00:18:46,340 --> 00:18:48,210
to remove files
that are similar.

373
00:18:48,210 --> 00:18:51,820
So we ended up with almost 3
terabytes of deduplicated data.

374
00:18:51,820 --> 00:18:54,370

375
00:18:54,370 --> 00:18:59,350
The stack comes also with a
very cool tool for opt out.

376
00:18:59,350 --> 00:19:02,570
This tool is basically a
space where you can go.

377
00:19:02,570 --> 00:19:04,330
You can type your
GitHub username

378
00:19:04,330 --> 00:19:07,330
and it tells you if you have
any of your GitHub repositories

379
00:19:07,330 --> 00:19:09,130
in the data set.

380
00:19:09,130 --> 00:19:11,200
And if that's the
case, there's also

381
00:19:11,200 --> 00:19:13,390
an option to fill
a form and request

382
00:19:13,390 --> 00:19:17,350
to be removed from all the
future trainings of BigCode.

383
00:19:17,350 --> 00:19:21,640
So we did that for the second
one, but also for the stack v2.

384
00:19:21,640 --> 00:19:24,790
In the v2 is a much
larger and enhanced

385
00:19:24,790 --> 00:19:27,550
data set compared to the v1.

386
00:19:27,550 --> 00:19:31,270
This time, instead of
cloning GitHub repositories,

387
00:19:31,270 --> 00:19:33,010
we went through
Software Heritage,

388
00:19:33,010 --> 00:19:34,690
which is an archive of code.

389
00:19:34,690 --> 00:19:38,800
They already did the scraping
and we just extracted the data

390
00:19:38,800 --> 00:19:39,970
from their archive.

391
00:19:39,970 --> 00:19:43,840
And we ended up, after
all the filtering,

392
00:19:43,840 --> 00:19:47,050
with almost 1
trillion tokens, which

393
00:19:47,050 --> 00:19:50,450
is a lot compared to the
v1 where we got around 200

394
00:19:50,450 --> 00:19:52,700
billion tokens at the end.

395
00:19:52,700 --> 00:19:57,200
We also added some high quality
resources like GitHub issues,

396
00:19:57,200 --> 00:20:00,260
math and coding data
sets, and pull requests.

397
00:20:00,260 --> 00:20:03,170
So these data sets, the
stack v1, the stack v2

398
00:20:03,170 --> 00:20:07,220
can be used to train LLMs on
code or to train general LLMs

399
00:20:07,220 --> 00:20:12,820
and include code as a subset
of the general web data.

400
00:20:12,820 --> 00:20:15,790
This shows how the stack
v2 compares to the v1.

401
00:20:15,790 --> 00:20:17,680
And you can see that
before filtering, it's

402
00:20:17,680 --> 00:20:20,560
almost 10 times larger
and after filtering, it's

403
00:20:20,560 --> 00:20:24,370
four or five times larger.

404
00:20:24,370 --> 00:20:27,970
So I talk about how to get web
data, how to get code data,

405
00:20:27,970 --> 00:20:30,770
and then I also
mentioned synthetic data.

406
00:20:30,770 --> 00:20:34,960
And it's this year and last year
that synthetic data became very

407
00:20:34,960 --> 00:20:37,100
important for LLM training.

408
00:20:37,100 --> 00:20:39,100
And I think that in
the next few years,

409
00:20:39,100 --> 00:20:41,170
it will become even
more important.

410
00:20:41,170 --> 00:20:44,500
And I think this was
mainly sparked by the five

411
00:20:44,500 --> 00:20:47,140
series of models by Microsoft.

412
00:20:47,140 --> 00:20:50,360
The first paper was called
textbooks are all you need.

413
00:20:50,360 --> 00:20:55,060
And they basically generated
synthetic textbooks using

414
00:20:55,060 --> 00:20:59,980
GPT-3.5 and GPT-4, and
they tried to build a new

415
00:20:59,980 --> 00:21:03,280
pre-training corpus that is
synthetic and they were able

416
00:21:03,280 --> 00:21:07,240
to match and outperform models
that are trained on web data

417
00:21:07,240 --> 00:21:08,680
sets.

418
00:21:08,680 --> 00:21:12,450
So this model was trained on
almost entirely synthetic data.

419
00:21:12,450 --> 00:21:15,050
But now, some of the
very popular LLMs

420
00:21:15,050 --> 00:21:19,200
are using synthetic data as
part of their pre-training mix.

421
00:21:19,200 --> 00:21:22,190
For example, Claude
3, in the model car,

422
00:21:22,190 --> 00:21:24,830
they say that they
generate data internally

423
00:21:24,830 --> 00:21:27,320
and they include it
in the pre-training.

424
00:21:27,320 --> 00:21:29,360
This is also the
case for Llama 3,

425
00:21:29,360 --> 00:21:32,690
where they use the LLMs
to build classifiers

426
00:21:32,690 --> 00:21:36,630
that would annotate samples and
only keep the high quality ones.

427
00:21:36,630 --> 00:21:39,290
But they also generated
synthetic content

428
00:21:39,290 --> 00:21:45,380
to improve performance on coding
and reasoning and long contexts.

429
00:21:45,380 --> 00:21:47,780
So synthetic data
is a very new topic,

430
00:21:47,780 --> 00:21:51,380
but it seems really interesting.

431
00:21:51,380 --> 00:21:54,150
I'm personally working also
on that as Hugging Face.

432
00:21:54,150 --> 00:21:57,830
We recently released a data
set called Cosmopedia, which

433
00:21:57,830 --> 00:22:02,120
was the largest data
set of synthetic texts,

434
00:22:02,120 --> 00:22:05,250
and it had almost
25 billion tokens.

435
00:22:05,250 --> 00:22:08,000
And instead of using
closed models like GPT-4,

436
00:22:08,000 --> 00:22:12,910
it used an open source
model, which is Mixtral-8x7B.

437
00:22:12,910 --> 00:22:15,790
And we also released
a blog post that

438
00:22:15,790 --> 00:22:18,970
explains how we
created this data set

439
00:22:18,970 --> 00:22:23,770
because it can be very tricky
to get very diverse samples.

440
00:22:23,770 --> 00:22:26,620
So we used an
approach where we had

441
00:22:26,620 --> 00:22:29,710
80% of the data that
comes from the web,

442
00:22:29,710 --> 00:22:32,500
and then we try to use
these web samples to build

443
00:22:32,500 --> 00:22:35,470
new prompts that ask models
to generate text books that

444
00:22:35,470 --> 00:22:37,450
are related to
these web samples,

445
00:22:37,450 --> 00:22:40,040
but while giving
them more context

446
00:22:40,040 --> 00:22:43,120
so we can limit the generations.

447
00:22:43,120 --> 00:22:47,680
For example, we can have a
topic that is a mathematics,

448
00:22:47,680 --> 00:22:50,690
and then we have web samples
that are related to mathematics.

449
00:22:50,690 --> 00:22:53,380
And each time we give the
model a prompt, generate

450
00:22:53,380 --> 00:22:55,840
a textbook in the field
of mathematics that

451
00:22:55,840 --> 00:22:57,830
is related to this web sample.

452
00:22:57,830 --> 00:23:01,840
And the more web samples we
add, the more diversity we add.

453
00:23:01,840 --> 00:23:05,290
We also use some curated
sources like Stanford courses

454
00:23:05,290 --> 00:23:08,710
and wikiHow, where we use
extracts from these pages

455
00:23:08,710 --> 00:23:12,830
to ask the models to generate
content that is related to them.

456
00:23:12,830 --> 00:23:15,800
You can find more details
in the Cosmopedia blog post.

457
00:23:15,800 --> 00:23:18,470

458
00:23:18,470 --> 00:23:20,420
So I guess now, we
also have the answer

459
00:23:20,420 --> 00:23:24,350
for our second question, which
was where to find the data.

460
00:23:24,350 --> 00:23:27,650
And if you're following,
we have one question left,

461
00:23:27,650 --> 00:23:30,480
which is, how can
we filter this data?

462
00:23:30,480 --> 00:23:33,590
Because for example, if you
use CommonCrawl and you need

463
00:23:33,590 --> 00:23:36,350
to filter it, and even
if you use the stack,

464
00:23:36,350 --> 00:23:39,120
we did not train our models
on the stack directly.

465
00:23:39,120 --> 00:23:42,380
We did a lot of filtering to
get a data set that is smaller

466
00:23:42,380 --> 00:23:45,980
but has a higher quality.

467
00:23:45,980 --> 00:23:49,520
And for this data set,
I will cite this slide

468
00:23:49,520 --> 00:23:52,575
from Thomas Wolfe's
presentation.

469
00:23:52,575 --> 00:23:54,450
This lecture is very
interesting, by the way.

470
00:23:54,450 --> 00:23:56,270
You can find it here.

471
00:23:56,270 --> 00:23:59,030
And this is from
the Yi paper, where

472
00:23:59,030 --> 00:24:02,090
they state that a
high quality data

473
00:24:02,090 --> 00:24:05,570
set might exhibit very
advanced capabilities

474
00:24:05,570 --> 00:24:07,580
for a standard architecture.

475
00:24:07,580 --> 00:24:12,980
And this is actually the
focus of many recent papers

476
00:24:12,980 --> 00:24:15,810
and we can see that
in model releases,

477
00:24:15,810 --> 00:24:18,470
the sections about data sets
are becoming smaller and smaller

478
00:24:18,470 --> 00:24:21,680
because people are realizing
that the data set is actually

479
00:24:21,680 --> 00:24:22,470
the backbone.

480
00:24:22,470 --> 00:24:25,790
It is the one that is making
some models much better

481
00:24:25,790 --> 00:24:26,790
than others.

482
00:24:26,790 --> 00:24:29,990
So it's really
important to spend

483
00:24:29,990 --> 00:24:32,480
a lot of time curating
these data sets

484
00:24:32,480 --> 00:24:35,570
and trying to remove all
the outliers and data

485
00:24:35,570 --> 00:24:37,795
sets that can hurt the
model during the training.

486
00:24:37,795 --> 00:24:41,150

487
00:24:41,150 --> 00:24:45,300
This is the pipeline from
the Yi paper for filtering

488
00:24:45,300 --> 00:24:47,910
their crawled web data sets.

489
00:24:47,910 --> 00:24:50,570
So first, they do
language filtering.

490
00:24:50,570 --> 00:24:53,300
So I guess in Yi's
case, they kept English

491
00:24:53,300 --> 00:24:54,870
and some Asian languages.

492
00:24:54,870 --> 00:24:57,380
Then they apply some
filtering techniques

493
00:24:57,380 --> 00:24:59,930
to remove low quality samples.

494
00:24:59,930 --> 00:25:02,300
For example, there
are some metrics,

495
00:25:02,300 --> 00:25:05,360
you look for files that
have a lot of lines repeated

496
00:25:05,360 --> 00:25:06,690
and then you remove them.

497
00:25:06,690 --> 00:25:08,580
There's also
rule-based collection.

498
00:25:08,580 --> 00:25:10,640
You also can use
perplexity filtering

499
00:25:10,640 --> 00:25:13,100
where you compute
something like loss

500
00:25:13,100 --> 00:25:15,660
and remove samples that
have a very high one.

501
00:25:15,660 --> 00:25:18,800
Then after that,
they also did a step

502
00:25:18,800 --> 00:25:22,190
which is very important,
deduplication,

503
00:25:22,190 --> 00:25:24,080
because there are a
lot of papers that

504
00:25:24,080 --> 00:25:26,570
study the effect of
duplicates on training

505
00:25:26,570 --> 00:25:29,960
and they find that skipping
duplicates in the training data

506
00:25:29,960 --> 00:25:34,220
can cause models to memorize
and they have less space

507
00:25:34,220 --> 00:25:35,960
to be creative.

508
00:25:35,960 --> 00:25:38,540
So this hurts the
performance of models

509
00:25:38,540 --> 00:25:42,030
and it's always advise
to remove duplicates.

510
00:25:42,030 --> 00:25:45,030
Using exact
duplication, to remove

511
00:25:45,030 --> 00:25:48,120
files that are exactly
identical, but also

512
00:25:48,120 --> 00:25:51,250
near deduplication to remove
files that are similar.

513
00:25:51,250 --> 00:25:55,500
And this uses techniques
like MinHash deduplication.

514
00:25:55,500 --> 00:25:58,740
For Yi, after that, they also
did more filtering on top

515
00:25:58,740 --> 00:26:01,200
like semantic and
topic filtering.

516
00:26:01,200 --> 00:26:04,200
But usually, you can do
the classic filtering

517
00:26:04,200 --> 00:26:09,590
and duplication and then be more
creative for the other filters.

518
00:26:09,590 --> 00:26:11,810
This was also the
case for FineWeb.

519
00:26:11,810 --> 00:26:14,840
The reason it is better
than other data sets is

520
00:26:14,840 --> 00:26:17,510
that because they spent
a lot of time trying

521
00:26:17,510 --> 00:26:21,680
to come up with better filters
and also deduplicate the data

522
00:26:21,680 --> 00:26:22,490
sets well.

523
00:26:22,490 --> 00:26:25,160

524
00:26:25,160 --> 00:26:28,860
Now the question is, OK,
we can do the duplication.

525
00:26:28,860 --> 00:26:32,240
I think we have methods that
are established to do that.

526
00:26:32,240 --> 00:26:35,120
We can also do
language filtering.

527
00:26:35,120 --> 00:26:38,810
But then if you want to filter
the data to remove garbage

528
00:26:38,810 --> 00:26:43,100
and lower quality files, how do
you come up with good filters?

529
00:26:43,100 --> 00:26:46,910
You can for sure find some
filters in the literature,

530
00:26:46,910 --> 00:26:49,280
but if you want to really
build a data set that

531
00:26:49,280 --> 00:26:51,410
is better than what
exists, you need

532
00:26:51,410 --> 00:26:53,900
to invest some time trying
to find more techniques

533
00:26:53,900 --> 00:26:55,820
that work better for your case.

534
00:26:55,820 --> 00:27:01,040
This can be done with manual
inspection, which is always

535
00:27:01,040 --> 00:27:03,680
a good idea to look at the
data and see what it actually

536
00:27:03,680 --> 00:27:04,620
looks like.

537
00:27:04,620 --> 00:27:08,480
And you can come up with filters
to help you during the training.

538
00:27:08,480 --> 00:27:11,180
But that is usually
not enough because you

539
00:27:11,180 --> 00:27:14,840
might have an intuition for
a filtering that works better

540
00:27:14,840 --> 00:27:15,828
for your model.

541
00:27:15,828 --> 00:27:17,870
But then when you train,
actually, this filtering

542
00:27:17,870 --> 00:27:18,810
doesn't help.

543
00:27:18,810 --> 00:27:23,040
And for example, for us, when
we were developing the StarCoder

544
00:27:23,040 --> 00:27:26,340
series of models, we
were thinking, OK,

545
00:27:26,340 --> 00:27:28,900
what are the best ways
for us to filter code?

546
00:27:28,900 --> 00:27:31,350
So we use some standard
filters, for example,

547
00:27:31,350 --> 00:27:33,270
to remove auto
generated content,

548
00:27:33,270 --> 00:27:35,220
but we try to come
up with a little bit

549
00:27:35,220 --> 00:27:38,610
more complex filtering
that could help us.

550
00:27:38,610 --> 00:27:40,890
Looking for files that
have a lot of comments

551
00:27:40,890 --> 00:27:43,890
because code that is
usually well documented

552
00:27:43,890 --> 00:27:47,700
is probably of a higher quality
than another code file that

553
00:27:47,700 --> 00:27:49,090
doesn't have any comments.

554
00:27:49,090 --> 00:27:51,210
So we implemented
this filter that

555
00:27:51,210 --> 00:27:53,970
looks for files that
have almost no comments,

556
00:27:53,970 --> 00:27:55,200
and then removes them.

557
00:27:55,200 --> 00:27:58,560
And we train the model
on that and turned out

558
00:27:58,560 --> 00:28:01,300
the performance improvement
was really negligible.

559
00:28:01,300 --> 00:28:03,300
It was not as much
as we thought.

560
00:28:03,300 --> 00:28:06,120
We also tried to use
another filter, which

561
00:28:06,120 --> 00:28:08,580
is using the stars
of a repository

562
00:28:08,580 --> 00:28:10,330
as an indicator of quality.

563
00:28:10,330 --> 00:28:13,860
So we tried removing all
the files from repository

564
00:28:13,860 --> 00:28:17,550
that have less than five stars,
and this ended up removing over

565
00:28:17,550 --> 00:28:19,510
70% of the data sets.

566
00:28:19,510 --> 00:28:21,360
And then when we
trained on it, the model

567
00:28:21,360 --> 00:28:24,630
was the worst model that we
trained in all our ablation

568
00:28:24,630 --> 00:28:27,940
experiments, simply because
it removed too much data.

569
00:28:27,940 --> 00:28:31,690
It was not worth using
this filtering technique.

570
00:28:31,690 --> 00:28:34,800
This is why it's very important
that when you have a filter,

571
00:28:34,800 --> 00:28:37,890
you should run what we
call an ablation model.

572
00:28:37,890 --> 00:28:40,800
The ablation is basically,
you take a subset of your data

573
00:28:40,800 --> 00:28:43,050
set after you
applied the filtering

574
00:28:43,050 --> 00:28:44,790
and you train a
small model on it

575
00:28:44,790 --> 00:28:48,510
and see how it behaves with
and without the filtering.

576
00:28:48,510 --> 00:28:52,320
And you might be wondering,
OK, if I use a small model,

577
00:28:52,320 --> 00:28:55,210
but does it really
extrapolate to larger models?

578
00:28:55,210 --> 00:28:56,650
I think that's a good question.

579
00:28:56,650 --> 00:28:58,830
But generally, from
our experience,

580
00:28:58,830 --> 00:29:00,990
we found that this
does extrapolate

581
00:29:00,990 --> 00:29:04,290
for most of data ablations.

582
00:29:04,290 --> 00:29:06,240
When you're doing
these ablations,

583
00:29:06,240 --> 00:29:10,470
you should select a set
of high signal benchmarks

584
00:29:10,470 --> 00:29:14,760
that could give you
some conclusions

585
00:29:14,760 --> 00:29:18,270
about the effect of your
filter early in the training.

586
00:29:18,270 --> 00:29:21,960
This can be some of the popular
NLP benchmarks for LLMs.

587
00:29:21,960 --> 00:29:25,050
For example, HellaSwag or MMLU.

588
00:29:25,050 --> 00:29:26,540
And you should also--

589
00:29:26,540 --> 00:29:29,750
sorry, here it's not tran,
it's train with different seeds

590
00:29:29,750 --> 00:29:31,820
to reduce the noise
because sometimes you

591
00:29:31,820 --> 00:29:34,160
can have filtering techniques
that don't give you

592
00:29:34,160 --> 00:29:35,430
a very big difference.

593
00:29:35,430 --> 00:29:39,480
But if you train with just one
seed, you must draw conclusions

594
00:29:39,480 --> 00:29:40,980
but they're actually just noise.

595
00:29:40,980 --> 00:29:43,550
So if you can and
you have the compute,

596
00:29:43,550 --> 00:29:46,530
it's always better to run
the same experiment with two

597
00:29:46,530 --> 00:29:48,560
or three different
seeds, and then

598
00:29:48,560 --> 00:29:50,360
maybe do something
like the averaging

599
00:29:50,360 --> 00:29:52,160
so that you reduce
the noise and you

600
00:29:52,160 --> 00:29:54,500
have more robust conclusions
about the effects

601
00:29:54,500 --> 00:29:57,920
of your filtering.

602
00:29:57,920 --> 00:30:01,010
For example, for the
fine web data set,

603
00:30:01,010 --> 00:30:05,040
the authors run over
200 plus ablations.

604
00:30:05,040 --> 00:30:08,210
These were 1 billion
models trained on, I think,

605
00:30:08,210 --> 00:30:09,810
30 billion tokens.

606
00:30:09,810 --> 00:30:13,490
And this is how they were able
to find filterings that worked

607
00:30:13,490 --> 00:30:15,625
better for their data sets.

608
00:30:15,625 --> 00:30:18,270

609
00:30:18,270 --> 00:30:22,020
Now let's go back to
our StarCoder use case

610
00:30:22,020 --> 00:30:25,630
and I will tell you about how
we filtered the stack data sets.

611
00:30:25,630 --> 00:30:29,280
So for the version
one, if you remember,

612
00:30:29,280 --> 00:30:32,610
we had 6 terabytes
of source code.

613
00:30:32,610 --> 00:30:34,950
But when we trained
StarCoder, we only

614
00:30:34,950 --> 00:30:38,440
used 800 gigabytes
of these 6 terabytes.

615
00:30:38,440 --> 00:30:44,460
So a lot of this data was
filtered out after our filtering

616
00:30:44,460 --> 00:30:46,080
or curation.

617
00:30:46,080 --> 00:30:49,080
The same happened for the
stack V2, where this time we

618
00:30:49,080 --> 00:30:52,710
started from 32 in 600
programming languages,

619
00:30:52,710 --> 00:30:54,930
and after the
filtering we ended up

620
00:30:54,930 --> 00:30:58,750
with only 6.3 terabytes of code.

621
00:30:58,750 --> 00:31:02,260
And for filtering
code, the approach

622
00:31:02,260 --> 00:31:05,650
is a bit similar to
just filtering web data,

623
00:31:05,650 --> 00:31:08,920
but the filtering techniques
are a bit different.

624
00:31:08,920 --> 00:31:13,300
So first, we wanted to include
a lot of programming languages

625
00:31:13,300 --> 00:31:16,490
and we looked at them and
we didn't keep all of them.

626
00:31:16,490 --> 00:31:19,660
We only kept the popular
ones and excluded,

627
00:31:19,660 --> 00:31:21,790
for example, configs
and languages that

628
00:31:21,790 --> 00:31:24,580
are no longer maintained,
so this was what V1.

629
00:31:24,580 --> 00:31:29,200
For StarCoder 2, we included
more languages over 600,

630
00:31:29,200 --> 00:31:31,270
and then we added
some other sources

631
00:31:31,270 --> 00:31:35,380
that could be interesting for
a code model to learn from,

632
00:31:35,380 --> 00:31:39,820
which are GitHub issues, Git
commits, and Jupyter Notebooks.

633
00:31:39,820 --> 00:31:44,140
We also added for the v2,
Kaggle, notebooks, and pull

634
00:31:44,140 --> 00:31:46,405
requests.

635
00:31:46,405 --> 00:31:48,430
Our second step
after we selected

636
00:31:48,430 --> 00:31:53,170
the languages we wanted to train
on was data quality inspection.

637
00:31:53,170 --> 00:31:54,940
So basically, as
I told you, we had

638
00:31:54,940 --> 00:31:58,120
some filters to remove
low quality files

639
00:31:58,120 --> 00:32:00,620
and autogenerated content.

640
00:32:00,620 --> 00:32:03,300
An example is the
average line length.

641
00:32:03,300 --> 00:32:06,552
So if we have an average
line length that is too high,

642
00:32:06,552 --> 00:32:08,510
there's probably something
wrong with this file

643
00:32:08,510 --> 00:32:10,340
or it's probably auto generated.

644
00:32:10,340 --> 00:32:14,840
But since we had almost
100 programming languages,

645
00:32:14,840 --> 00:32:17,000
we should not use
the same threshold

646
00:32:17,000 --> 00:32:18,830
for all the languages
for this filter

647
00:32:18,830 --> 00:32:20,690
because some programming
languages just

648
00:32:20,690 --> 00:32:21,990
have longer lines.

649
00:32:21,990 --> 00:32:24,350
So it's important to
do some inspection

650
00:32:24,350 --> 00:32:26,790
and look at some samples
from these languages.

651
00:32:26,790 --> 00:32:29,520
In our case, we had
the BigCode community

652
00:32:29,520 --> 00:32:33,440
which helps us look at
100 samples per extension

653
00:32:33,440 --> 00:32:36,440
and derive the appropriate
thresholds and filtering

654
00:32:36,440 --> 00:32:37,146
heuristics.

655
00:32:37,146 --> 00:32:39,970

656
00:32:39,970 --> 00:32:44,080
The third deduplicate--
sorry, filtering step

657
00:32:44,080 --> 00:32:46,720
was near-deduplication.

658
00:32:46,720 --> 00:32:50,260
We found that near-deduplication
was the filtering that gave us

659
00:32:50,260 --> 00:32:52,190
the most performance boost.

660
00:32:52,190 --> 00:32:55,970
It's also very easy to apply
because it's language agnostic.

661
00:32:55,970 --> 00:32:58,480
Even though we have 86
programming languages,

662
00:32:58,480 --> 00:33:01,370
we don't need to change the
duplication for each language.

663
00:33:01,370 --> 00:33:03,550
We can just apply
it to the whole sub,

664
00:33:03,550 --> 00:33:05,170
to the whole data set.

665
00:33:05,170 --> 00:33:08,920
And here I show you some
results of the effects

666
00:33:08,920 --> 00:33:10,320
of the duplication.

667
00:33:10,320 --> 00:33:13,790
For example, here you can see
this model Python all licensed.

668
00:33:13,790 --> 00:33:16,120
If the filtering is
done, you get a pass

669
00:33:16,120 --> 00:33:19,040
at one, which is our
code metric of 13.

670
00:33:19,040 --> 00:33:22,930
But if you apply no duplication,
you go from 13 to 17.

671
00:33:22,930 --> 00:33:25,580
That's a very big
performance bump.

672
00:33:25,580 --> 00:33:28,780
The same goes for other subsets
like permissive license.

673
00:33:28,780 --> 00:33:33,560
So we decided to use
deduplication for our data set

674
00:33:33,560 --> 00:33:36,310
and to use strong
deduplication to really remove

675
00:33:36,310 --> 00:33:40,180
all the files that
could be similar.

676
00:33:40,180 --> 00:33:42,580
Another step is
in our pipeline is

677
00:33:42,580 --> 00:33:45,710
to remove personal
identifiable information.

678
00:33:45,710 --> 00:33:49,990
So this could be names,
emails, or keys, or passwords

679
00:33:49,990 --> 00:33:52,460
because we scraped
code from GitHub.

680
00:33:52,460 --> 00:33:55,120
And although GitHub
has some tools

681
00:33:55,120 --> 00:33:58,150
to detect secrets and
prompt users to remove them,

682
00:33:58,150 --> 00:33:59,955
that's not always the case.

683
00:33:59,955 --> 00:34:01,330
And we found that
there was still

684
00:34:01,330 --> 00:34:03,340
a lot of secrets
in the data sets.

685
00:34:03,340 --> 00:34:05,320
And we train a
model, you don't want

686
00:34:05,320 --> 00:34:08,949
it to be trained on that because
in inference, it might generate

687
00:34:08,949 --> 00:34:11,530
sensitive or personal data.

688
00:34:11,530 --> 00:34:15,429
So our approach to removing it
was to first annotate the data

689
00:34:15,429 --> 00:34:17,830
sets for PII.

690
00:34:17,830 --> 00:34:21,340
We collaborated with
an annotation company

691
00:34:21,340 --> 00:34:23,620
to annotate some samples.

692
00:34:23,620 --> 00:34:27,880
So the annotators were
tasked with the labeling PII

693
00:34:27,880 --> 00:34:28,940
when they found it.

694
00:34:28,940 --> 00:34:31,610
For example, if they find the
name, they give it a class name.

695
00:34:31,610 --> 00:34:34,190
If you find an email, they
also label it as an email.

696
00:34:34,190 --> 00:34:37,159
So it was a named
entity recognition task.

697
00:34:37,159 --> 00:34:39,880
And then we trained
the StarPII, which

698
00:34:39,880 --> 00:34:42,940
is our NER model
to detect this PII,

699
00:34:42,940 --> 00:34:48,639
and then we run it on the
whole StarCoder training data.

700
00:34:48,639 --> 00:34:53,980
This took almost 800 GPU
hours because it's a more--

701
00:34:53,980 --> 00:34:57,760
it's a neural network and
it needs to run on GPUs.

702
00:34:57,760 --> 00:35:02,620
The last step in our filtering
was data decontamination

703
00:35:02,620 --> 00:35:06,370
because you should make sure to
remove the benchmarks and test

704
00:35:06,370 --> 00:35:08,090
sets from your training data.

705
00:35:08,090 --> 00:35:11,990
Otherwise, your evaluation
numbers will just be inflated.

706
00:35:11,990 --> 00:35:14,260
So we made sure to
remove the benchmarks

707
00:35:14,260 --> 00:35:16,950
that we use for evaluation
from our training sets.

708
00:35:16,950 --> 00:35:19,810

709
00:35:19,810 --> 00:35:23,410
The last step in the data
curation of the stack

710
00:35:23,410 --> 00:35:25,550
was to format the data.

711
00:35:25,550 --> 00:35:28,720
So now that the data is
filtered and because code

712
00:35:28,720 --> 00:35:31,540
is different from
text, we can allow

713
00:35:31,540 --> 00:35:35,590
ourselves to apply some
nice formatting that

714
00:35:35,590 --> 00:35:37,240
could help us during inference.

715
00:35:37,240 --> 00:35:40,750
For example, for StarCoder,
we had the code file,

716
00:35:40,750 --> 00:35:43,780
but before the code,
we added some tokens

717
00:35:43,780 --> 00:35:46,150
that indicate that this
is the repository name

718
00:35:46,150 --> 00:35:49,180
and another token file name
that indicates the file

719
00:35:49,180 --> 00:35:51,100
name and another one for stars.

720
00:35:51,100 --> 00:35:55,750
And this is interesting because
this model resembles StarCoder

721
00:35:55,750 --> 00:35:57,320
and other code models.

722
00:35:57,320 --> 00:36:00,610
I guess their main use case
is to be plugged in an IDE,

723
00:36:00,610 --> 00:36:02,210
for example, VS Code.

724
00:36:02,210 --> 00:36:05,170
And when you're
using them, it could

725
00:36:05,170 --> 00:36:07,390
be interesting to
append the code

726
00:36:07,390 --> 00:36:10,660
file with the name of
the file, for example,

727
00:36:10,660 --> 00:36:12,820
I don't know, file.py
so that the model would

728
00:36:12,820 --> 00:36:14,380
know this is the Python file.

729
00:36:14,380 --> 00:36:17,120
If it's in another language,
when you add the file name

730
00:36:17,120 --> 00:36:18,620
and you have the
extension, it could

731
00:36:18,620 --> 00:36:21,710
know that this is detect
the language that it

732
00:36:21,710 --> 00:36:23,150
should generate code in.

733
00:36:23,150 --> 00:36:26,930
We also added GitHub stars
token and we try to play with it

734
00:36:26,930 --> 00:36:30,230
to say, "This file
has the 100 stars."

735
00:36:30,230 --> 00:36:32,570
And see if the model would
generate higher quality

736
00:36:32,570 --> 00:36:36,470
code than if it were to
generate for zero stars.

737
00:36:36,470 --> 00:36:38,990
We didn't find any differences
really during inference,

738
00:36:38,990 --> 00:36:43,010
but it was fun to add
all this formatting.

739
00:36:43,010 --> 00:36:46,130
For StarCoder2, one
of the improvements

740
00:36:46,130 --> 00:36:50,270
was that StarCoder2
was repository aware.

741
00:36:50,270 --> 00:36:53,870
Because when we have GitHub
repositories, it's repository.

742
00:36:53,870 --> 00:36:56,840
So we have some files that are
in the same repository that

743
00:36:56,840 --> 00:36:58,440
are related to each other.

744
00:36:58,440 --> 00:37:01,370
But when we built
the stack V1, we just

745
00:37:01,370 --> 00:37:03,680
shuffled files so
we kept-- we didn't

746
00:37:03,680 --> 00:37:05,480
keep this repository structure.

747
00:37:05,480 --> 00:37:07,950
And we trained the model,
we just shuffled them

748
00:37:07,950 --> 00:37:11,030
and the model did not
know if two files belong

749
00:37:11,030 --> 00:37:12,510
to the same repository.

750
00:37:12,510 --> 00:37:14,690
But when we did
StarCoder2, we try

751
00:37:14,690 --> 00:37:17,190
to keep files that are
in the same repository

752
00:37:17,190 --> 00:37:18,300
next to each other.

753
00:37:18,300 --> 00:37:20,870
And how we did that is
by concatenating them

754
00:37:20,870 --> 00:37:23,840
with some special tokens like
file SEP, which basically

755
00:37:23,840 --> 00:37:24,960
separates files.

756
00:37:24,960 --> 00:37:29,150
And this way, the
model can know which

757
00:37:29,150 --> 00:37:31,280
files need the same
repository and try

758
00:37:31,280 --> 00:37:32,750
to find links between them.

759
00:37:32,750 --> 00:37:36,650
3D parallelism, and then you
have also LightEval for doing

760
00:37:36,650 --> 00:37:37,770
the evaluation.

761
00:37:37,770 --> 00:37:41,390
So this is a stack to be able
to run your full trainings,

762
00:37:41,390 --> 00:37:43,560
but also your ablation models.

763
00:37:43,560 --> 00:37:45,590
You can apply a
filter from datatrove

764
00:37:45,590 --> 00:37:48,530
and then train with nanotron
and evaluate with LightEval,

765
00:37:48,530 --> 00:37:50,390
and they're well
integrated together

766
00:37:50,390 --> 00:37:52,610
and they make one ecosystem.

767
00:37:52,610 --> 00:37:54,930
So that's for general LLMs.

768
00:37:54,930 --> 00:37:58,250
For code LLMs, we
also released the code

769
00:37:58,250 --> 00:38:01,580
we used for both the stack
and StarCoder models,

770
00:38:01,580 --> 00:38:04,180
and our BigCode
repository on GitHub.

771
00:38:04,180 --> 00:38:07,320

772
00:38:07,320 --> 00:38:10,950
And I think we just answered
our third question, which

773
00:38:10,950 --> 00:38:12,600
was how to filter the data.

774
00:38:12,600 --> 00:38:16,200
So now, you know how to--
first, how much data you need,

775
00:38:16,200 --> 00:38:20,340
and then where you can get
this data, both web, and code,

776
00:38:20,340 --> 00:38:21,960
and synthetic, and curated.

777
00:38:21,960 --> 00:38:25,500
And you also know how you
can properly filter the data

778
00:38:25,500 --> 00:38:27,990
and you can test the
filtering techniques

779
00:38:27,990 --> 00:38:30,690
that you have in mind.

780
00:38:30,690 --> 00:38:34,980
So now, let me tell you a
little bit more about code LLMs

781
00:38:34,980 --> 00:38:37,860
because that's what
I'm working on.

782
00:38:37,860 --> 00:38:41,280
And I'm trying to give you
a little bit of an overview

783
00:38:41,280 --> 00:38:44,460
about these models so that you
know how to train good LLMs,

784
00:38:44,460 --> 00:38:48,090
but you also know how to build
very cool code assistants

785
00:38:48,090 --> 00:38:51,450
and completion models.

786
00:38:51,450 --> 00:38:54,540
So how all of this
started was when

787
00:38:54,540 --> 00:38:56,820
GitHub Copilot was released.

788
00:38:56,820 --> 00:39:00,060
And it was very interesting
because it was so much better

789
00:39:00,060 --> 00:39:02,460
than all the other code
completion models that

790
00:39:02,460 --> 00:39:06,770
were before it, which were very
small and much less performance.

791
00:39:06,770 --> 00:39:11,950
And GitHub Copilot was using
the Codex model by OpenAI.

792
00:39:11,950 --> 00:39:15,040
And they just showed that
you can train or code LLM

793
00:39:15,040 --> 00:39:18,970
in the same way that you train
an for English, for example.

794
00:39:18,970 --> 00:39:21,580
You can just take a
large transformer model

795
00:39:21,580 --> 00:39:25,600
and give it a lot of code data
and it will learn this code.

796
00:39:25,600 --> 00:39:27,250
Because before, a
lot of people were

797
00:39:27,250 --> 00:39:30,220
trying to treat code very
differently, for example,

798
00:39:30,220 --> 00:39:32,950
by using abstract syntax trees.

799
00:39:32,950 --> 00:39:35,050
But what Codex model
showed is that you

800
00:39:35,050 --> 00:39:36,580
can treat code like text.

801
00:39:36,580 --> 00:39:38,740
And if you want to
predict the next line,

802
00:39:38,740 --> 00:39:41,110
you can predict the
next text, you just

803
00:39:41,110 --> 00:39:44,520
do next token prediction
and you get your code.

804
00:39:44,520 --> 00:39:48,510
It works very well, much better
compared to the more feature

805
00:39:48,510 --> 00:39:50,580
engineering techniques.

806
00:39:50,580 --> 00:39:54,570
And that was over two
years ago and we didn't

807
00:39:54,570 --> 00:39:57,330
have any good open code models.

808
00:39:57,330 --> 00:40:00,960
But today, if you go to the hub,
you can find that we have over

809
00:40:00,960 --> 00:40:04,380
1,700 models that
are trained on code.

810
00:40:04,380 --> 00:40:07,620
So these are models that
are either trained only

811
00:40:07,620 --> 00:40:12,840
on code or LLMs that included
code as part of their training.

812
00:40:12,840 --> 00:40:15,810
So you can see that we've
made a lot of progress

813
00:40:15,810 --> 00:40:21,060
in this code generation
field, which is amazing.

814
00:40:21,060 --> 00:40:26,160
And this is the word-- this is
the result of the committee's

815
00:40:26,160 --> 00:40:29,580
work to build very good
instruction-tuned models

816
00:40:29,580 --> 00:40:31,130
and base models.

817
00:40:31,130 --> 00:40:33,540
For example, here, as you
can see in the leaderboard,

818
00:40:33,540 --> 00:40:37,530
we have some very strong
models that score almost 80%

819
00:40:37,530 --> 00:40:39,750
on the code evaluation
benchmark, which

820
00:40:39,750 --> 00:40:42,690
is human eval, which
means they get almost 80%

821
00:40:42,690 --> 00:40:47,110
of the problems right, which
is a very large number.

822
00:40:47,110 --> 00:40:51,100
And when talking about the
landscape of open code LLMs,

823
00:40:51,100 --> 00:40:54,250
in BigCode, we have
released the stack data set,

824
00:40:54,250 --> 00:40:56,350
which is now the default
data set for training

825
00:40:56,350 --> 00:41:00,790
on code, and also StarCoder1 and
StarCoder2 two family of models,

826
00:41:00,790 --> 00:41:03,610
and other instruction-tuned
models with the H2 team

827
00:41:03,610 --> 00:41:05,770
like StarChat2.

828
00:41:05,770 --> 00:41:09,250
Meta also released some very
good code models which are

829
00:41:09,250 --> 00:41:13,630
the code Llama series of
models that go from 7B to 70B.

830
00:41:13,630 --> 00:41:17,920
There are also the deep C models
which are also very strong,

831
00:41:17,920 --> 00:41:21,010
and they have also other models
like the recent Granite models

832
00:41:21,010 --> 00:41:25,250
from IBM, CodeQwen,
CodeGen, and StableCode.

833
00:41:25,250 --> 00:41:28,990
So there are different
providers for code LLMs

834
00:41:28,990 --> 00:41:32,750
and also for data sets for code.

835
00:41:32,750 --> 00:41:36,440
And the main reason we started
the BigCode collaboration

836
00:41:36,440 --> 00:41:40,610
and to train our models was
to have a collaboration where

837
00:41:40,610 --> 00:41:43,550
we have full data transparency.

838
00:41:43,550 --> 00:41:45,920
We released all the
details about the training,

839
00:41:45,920 --> 00:41:48,350
but also the data is
public so that people

840
00:41:48,350 --> 00:41:50,430
can inspect it and use it.

841
00:41:50,430 --> 00:41:53,570
And we also have the code for
the processing and the model

842
00:41:53,570 --> 00:41:54,270
weights.

843
00:41:54,270 --> 00:41:55,950
And the collaboration was open.

844
00:41:55,950 --> 00:41:59,750
We had over 1,000 researchers
join our Slack and following

845
00:41:59,750 --> 00:42:02,470
the journey with us.

846
00:42:02,470 --> 00:42:05,350
And this created a
BigCode ecosystem

847
00:42:05,350 --> 00:42:07,960
where the stack was
used in the pre-training

848
00:42:07,960 --> 00:42:10,210
of a lot of
prominent code models

849
00:42:10,210 --> 00:42:13,070
like CodeGen and StableCode.

850
00:42:13,070 --> 00:42:15,430
And the StarCoder
models were used

851
00:42:15,430 --> 00:42:18,240
as basis for a lot of
community fine tuning.

852
00:42:18,240 --> 00:42:20,910

853
00:42:20,910 --> 00:42:23,940
And I think it's
very important to be

854
00:42:23,940 --> 00:42:28,260
aware of what makes
a release of an LLM,

855
00:42:28,260 --> 00:42:30,660
whether it be a code
LLM or general LLM

856
00:42:30,660 --> 00:42:32,940
open and responsible.

857
00:42:32,940 --> 00:42:35,220
And I think this is four-fold.

858
00:42:35,220 --> 00:42:38,460
First, it's really
good for the community

859
00:42:38,460 --> 00:42:41,260
and for research
in AI in general.

860
00:42:41,260 --> 00:42:46,170
If you can make open access
data sets, this will help.

861
00:42:46,170 --> 00:42:48,810
This will mean having
data inspection tools,

862
00:42:48,810 --> 00:42:53,160
but also opt out tools to
respect people's wishes

863
00:42:53,160 --> 00:42:55,000
regarding their data sets.

864
00:42:55,000 --> 00:42:57,840
For example, if they don't want
to be included in the trainings,

865
00:42:57,840 --> 00:43:00,060
they should be able to opt out.

866
00:43:00,060 --> 00:43:02,790
It's also important to
remove personal identifiable

867
00:43:02,790 --> 00:43:04,380
information.

868
00:43:04,380 --> 00:43:07,890
So an open release does not mean
just releasing model weights

869
00:43:07,890 --> 00:43:11,550
and stopping there, but also
making your work reproducible

870
00:43:11,550 --> 00:43:15,270
by fully documenting the
pipeline for using these models

871
00:43:15,270 --> 00:43:20,590
and also releasing tools for
evaluation and technical reports

872
00:43:20,590 --> 00:43:24,010
that documents the
whole pipeline.

873
00:43:24,010 --> 00:43:28,210
And for us in BigCode,
we went from SantaCoder,

874
00:43:28,210 --> 00:43:31,450
which was part of our
ablations to understand how

875
00:43:31,450 --> 00:43:33,820
to filter the stack data sets.

876
00:43:33,820 --> 00:43:37,030
And then we went to StarCoder
which was released last year,

877
00:43:37,030 --> 00:43:39,290
a 15 billion code
generation model.

878
00:43:39,290 --> 00:43:42,610
And then this year,
we released StarCoder2

879
00:43:42,610 --> 00:43:45,340
which was trained on much
more programming languages

880
00:43:45,340 --> 00:43:51,080
and had a much higher
evaluation score.

881
00:43:51,080 --> 00:43:55,790
And StarCoder was also rated
as the most transparent model

882
00:43:55,790 --> 00:43:58,730
by the Stanford Foundation
model transparency

883
00:43:58,730 --> 00:44:01,520
index, which is really
heartwarming given

884
00:44:01,520 --> 00:44:04,190
the efforts that we put
into data governance

885
00:44:04,190 --> 00:44:06,560
and into making the
model release as

886
00:44:06,560 --> 00:44:07,735
transparent as possible.

887
00:44:07,735 --> 00:44:10,950

888
00:44:10,950 --> 00:44:14,700
Regarding evaluation, so
for example, StarCoder 15B,

889
00:44:14,700 --> 00:44:19,000
when it was released, it was
the state of the art code model.

890
00:44:19,000 --> 00:44:23,340
And this was also the case for
StarCoder 215B among other 15B

891
00:44:23,340 --> 00:44:24,340
models.

892
00:44:24,340 --> 00:44:28,980
And it was even close or
better than larger models.

893
00:44:28,980 --> 00:44:31,770
I think I don't have the plot
here, but it was better than--

894
00:44:31,770 --> 00:44:37,230
it was matching code Llama 34B,
and it was close to DeepSeek 33B

895
00:44:37,230 --> 00:44:38,530
on some benchmarks.

896
00:44:38,530 --> 00:44:41,160
And here, for example,
you can see the results

897
00:44:41,160 --> 00:44:45,060
on different benchmarks because
when releasing our model,

898
00:44:45,060 --> 00:44:47,370
it's really important
that you don't just

899
00:44:47,370 --> 00:44:50,400
evaluate on one benchmark,
but you should add

900
00:44:50,400 --> 00:44:52,080
as many benchmarks as you want.

901
00:44:52,080 --> 00:44:54,750
In case you had
contamination, although we

902
00:44:54,750 --> 00:44:57,570
try to avoid this one benchmark,
there's a very low chance

903
00:44:57,570 --> 00:45:00,450
that you also have contamination
on other benchmarks.

904
00:45:00,450 --> 00:45:03,150
And it also allows you
to fully understand

905
00:45:03,150 --> 00:45:08,680
how your model behaves if you
add more evaluation benchmarks.

906
00:45:08,680 --> 00:45:11,440
And I think that's just a good
practice that everyone should

907
00:45:11,440 --> 00:45:12,795
be doing with their releases.

908
00:45:12,795 --> 00:45:15,670

909
00:45:15,670 --> 00:45:19,900
So with the StarCoder models,
we also released some tooling

910
00:45:19,900 --> 00:45:24,850
like VS Code implementation,
which also has a membership

911
00:45:24,850 --> 00:45:28,810
test that tries to see
if the generated code was

912
00:45:28,810 --> 00:45:32,240
in the training data and
highlight that to the author.

913
00:45:32,240 --> 00:45:36,160
So that's part of
our code attribution

914
00:45:36,160 --> 00:45:40,730
efforts for these code models.

915
00:45:40,730 --> 00:45:43,760
Maybe you're interested in
using these models to build

916
00:45:43,760 --> 00:45:47,060
your own personal
Copilot and fine tuning

917
00:45:47,060 --> 00:45:50,330
StarCoder or Code Llama or other
models on your personal code

918
00:45:50,330 --> 00:45:51,380
bases.

919
00:45:51,380 --> 00:45:55,850
To do that, there's a very nice
blog post by Sourab and Sayak,

920
00:45:55,850 --> 00:45:58,400
where they try to
take a code model

921
00:45:58,400 --> 00:46:02,630
and train it on the Hugging
Face internal libraries,

922
00:46:02,630 --> 00:46:07,200
and then deploy it in old Llama
and have a local code assistant.

923
00:46:07,200 --> 00:46:10,470
And the pipeline is very similar
to what we did in pre-training.

924
00:46:10,470 --> 00:46:12,410
First, you take
your data set, you

925
00:46:12,410 --> 00:46:15,080
try to filter out the things
you don't want to keep,

926
00:46:15,080 --> 00:46:18,010
and then you do deduplication,
and you train your model.

927
00:46:18,010 --> 00:46:20,270
So in this case, it will
be just a fine tuning,

928
00:46:20,270 --> 00:46:21,750
so it will be much quicker.

929
00:46:21,750 --> 00:46:24,470
You can use libraries
like PEFT which

930
00:46:24,470 --> 00:46:27,350
do parameter efficient fine
tuning where you don't need

931
00:46:27,350 --> 00:46:29,360
to train all the
parameters of your models,

932
00:46:29,360 --> 00:46:32,870
but you only inject few
trainable parameters.

933
00:46:32,870 --> 00:46:35,660
This makes the
training much faster.

934
00:46:35,660 --> 00:46:39,136
For example, 7B model can be
trained in a Google Colab.

935
00:46:39,136 --> 00:46:41,730

936
00:46:41,730 --> 00:46:43,920
Now, let's go back
to evaluation.

937
00:46:43,920 --> 00:46:46,260
So, for example,
for LLMs, there's

938
00:46:46,260 --> 00:46:50,160
the open LLM leaderboard
that evaluates models.

939
00:46:50,160 --> 00:46:54,060
There's also the LLMs arena,
which compares instructs models

940
00:46:54,060 --> 00:46:56,490
and uses human evaluation.

941
00:46:56,490 --> 00:46:59,610
For code models, one of
the most popular benchmarks

942
00:46:59,610 --> 00:47:00,930
is human eval.

943
00:47:00,930 --> 00:47:03,150
And it's basically
a benchmark where

944
00:47:03,150 --> 00:47:08,800
you have a function that the
model has to autocomplete.

945
00:47:08,800 --> 00:47:11,100
And then when the
function is completed,

946
00:47:11,100 --> 00:47:15,450
you take this solution and then
you run it against multiple unit

947
00:47:15,450 --> 00:47:18,600
tests and you count
how many solutions pass

948
00:47:18,600 --> 00:47:20,080
and how many solutions fail.

949
00:47:20,080 --> 00:47:23,800
And then you count what we a
metric that we call pass at one.

950
00:47:23,800 --> 00:47:25,470
For example, this
is the one that's

951
00:47:25,470 --> 00:47:27,480
been reported in
this leaderboard

952
00:47:27,480 --> 00:47:30,330
and this gives you
the human eval score.

953
00:47:30,330 --> 00:47:32,670
There's also a translation
of this benchmark

954
00:47:32,670 --> 00:47:34,590
to 18 other languages.

955
00:47:34,590 --> 00:47:38,280
Here, I show Java and
JavaScript and C+ plus.

956
00:47:38,280 --> 00:47:40,620
And this benchmark
is called MultiPL-E.

957
00:47:40,620 --> 00:47:44,950
So it allows you to see
how well each model does

958
00:47:44,950 --> 00:47:48,130
on which programming language
and choose the one that's

959
00:47:48,130 --> 00:47:51,630
the most interesting for you.

960
00:47:51,630 --> 00:47:55,680
But these benchmarks
usually have an issue

961
00:47:55,680 --> 00:48:00,180
of contamination and
overfitting, especially

962
00:48:00,180 --> 00:48:01,810
instruction-tuned models.

963
00:48:01,810 --> 00:48:04,680
I don't know if you've already
checked what these data sets

964
00:48:04,680 --> 00:48:07,440
look like, but
usually for code, they

965
00:48:07,440 --> 00:48:11,850
are an instruction that asks the
model to generate an exercise.

966
00:48:11,850 --> 00:48:13,680
And often, if you
look at them, they

967
00:48:13,680 --> 00:48:15,780
look really similar
to human eval which

968
00:48:15,780 --> 00:48:18,280
is function implementations.

969
00:48:18,280 --> 00:48:22,230
So there's a very high chance
of having contamination,

970
00:48:22,230 --> 00:48:25,830
which means having
some files that

971
00:48:25,830 --> 00:48:30,610
look like human eval exercises
in your instruction tuning data

972
00:48:30,610 --> 00:48:31,950
set.

973
00:48:31,950 --> 00:48:35,760
So here, for example, this
plot is from the LiveCodeBench

974
00:48:35,760 --> 00:48:40,320
leaderboard, and they find
that some benchmarks may

975
00:48:40,320 --> 00:48:42,960
be overfitting on human eval.

976
00:48:42,960 --> 00:48:46,710
And so their solution was
to have a leaderboard called

977
00:48:46,710 --> 00:48:51,280
LiveCodeBench where
they regularly scrape

978
00:48:51,280 --> 00:48:56,780
new problems from platforms like
code contests and leaked code.

979
00:48:56,780 --> 00:48:58,810
And they evaluate
the models only

980
00:48:58,810 --> 00:49:02,210
on the problems that were
released after the model release

981
00:49:02,210 --> 00:49:02,710
date.

982
00:49:02,710 --> 00:49:06,910
This way, they are sure that
there is no contamination.

983
00:49:06,910 --> 00:49:09,260
And for example, that
was the case here.

984
00:49:09,260 --> 00:49:12,820
They tried to evaluate these
models on all the data they

985
00:49:12,820 --> 00:49:15,280
have, and then they can
compare the performance

986
00:49:15,280 --> 00:49:18,600
to the data that was only
released after the model

987
00:49:18,600 --> 00:49:19,100
release.

988
00:49:19,100 --> 00:49:21,070
And they found that
some models were not

989
00:49:21,070 --> 00:49:24,100
consistent in their results.

990
00:49:24,100 --> 00:49:27,020
So that's one interesting
thing to keep in mind.

991
00:49:27,020 --> 00:49:31,210
And this is also
another leader for this

992
00:49:31,210 --> 00:49:34,900
will be interesting to compare
not just open models, but also

993
00:49:34,900 --> 00:49:38,260
closed models like GPT-4 and
see where the open source

994
00:49:38,260 --> 00:49:42,900
community is standing
compared to these code models.

995
00:49:42,900 --> 00:49:44,620
So that was my presentation.

996
00:49:44,620 --> 00:49:47,350
Thank you very much
for your attention.

997
00:49:47,350 --> 00:49:52,230
And if you have any
questions, I can answer them.

998
00:49:52,230 --> 00:49:55,920
Yes, thank you very much for
the great insightful talk.

999
00:49:55,920 --> 00:49:59,120
So we have some
questions here on Slido.

1000
00:49:59,120 --> 00:50:02,340
So I'm not sure if there are
any in-person questions or else

1001
00:50:02,340 --> 00:50:05,740
I will get started with
the Slido question.

1002
00:50:05,740 --> 00:50:10,460

1003
00:50:10,460 --> 00:50:11,980
Sure.

1004
00:50:11,980 --> 00:50:12,860
OK, guess not.

1005
00:50:12,860 --> 00:50:16,630
So I'll ask some of
the questions online.

1006
00:50:16,630 --> 00:50:20,340
I think I had submitted
some of these as well.

1007
00:50:20,340 --> 00:50:23,380
It seems like there's some
questions about synthetic data.

1008
00:50:23,380 --> 00:50:25,650
Let me see.

1009
00:50:25,650 --> 00:50:27,960
I was also wondering about this.

1010
00:50:27,960 --> 00:50:30,210
So someone's asking what
are the consequences

1011
00:50:30,210 --> 00:50:35,350
of training AI models on
AI-generated synthetic data?

1012
00:50:35,350 --> 00:50:37,930
Do you foresee any
problems with this?

1013
00:50:37,930 --> 00:50:40,020
And there's a related question.

1014
00:50:40,020 --> 00:50:42,840
Does synthetic data
closely represent

1015
00:50:42,840 --> 00:50:44,590
the natural distribution
of language.

1016
00:50:44,590 --> 00:50:47,160
I assume some low
quality data from humans

1017
00:50:47,160 --> 00:50:50,460
is necessary for things
like learning robustness

1018
00:50:50,460 --> 00:50:52,410
and so forth.

1019
00:50:52,410 --> 00:50:53,210
Yeah, sure.

1020
00:50:53,210 --> 00:50:55,170
These are very great questions.

1021
00:50:55,170 --> 00:50:58,680
So about the consequences
of training models

1022
00:50:58,680 --> 00:51:02,110
on AI-generated data, I
can think of two main ones.

1023
00:51:02,110 --> 00:51:06,330
First is enforcing some
biases because models already

1024
00:51:06,330 --> 00:51:07,450
have some biases.

1025
00:51:07,450 --> 00:51:09,900
And if we train on data
that is generated by them,

1026
00:51:09,900 --> 00:51:12,480
we might be enforcing
it even more.

1027
00:51:12,480 --> 00:51:15,570
The other thing is, for
example, contamination.

1028
00:51:15,570 --> 00:51:17,730
These models might be trained--

1029
00:51:17,730 --> 00:51:20,070
might generate content that
looks like the evaluation

1030
00:51:20,070 --> 00:51:20,890
benchmarks.

1031
00:51:20,890 --> 00:51:22,620
And when you train on
that, you will have

1032
00:51:22,620 --> 00:51:24,360
contamination in your data.

1033
00:51:24,360 --> 00:51:27,090
So, for example, one of the
critiques of the five model

1034
00:51:27,090 --> 00:51:29,970
is that people, because they
did not see the synthetic data

1035
00:51:29,970 --> 00:51:32,070
and the models were very
good on the benchmarks,

1036
00:51:32,070 --> 00:51:33,460
they were very skeptical.

1037
00:51:33,460 --> 00:51:36,030
Are these models really good
or are they just overfitting

1038
00:51:36,030 --> 00:51:37,350
on the benchmarks?

1039
00:51:37,350 --> 00:51:39,510
So I think contamination
and enforcing

1040
00:51:39,510 --> 00:51:43,590
biases are one of the main
things to keep in mind.

1041
00:51:43,590 --> 00:51:47,580
And regarding synthetic
data not being

1042
00:51:47,580 --> 00:51:50,550
the same as a web
distribution, I

1043
00:51:50,550 --> 00:51:52,460
think that's a very good point.

1044
00:51:52,460 --> 00:51:56,290
And for example, when we
were developing Cosmopedia,

1045
00:51:56,290 --> 00:51:59,740
first, we found that it
was worse than the web,

1046
00:51:59,740 --> 00:52:03,580
and which was surprising because
we spent a lot of time trying

1047
00:52:03,580 --> 00:52:05,830
to curate this data
set which looks so much

1048
00:52:05,830 --> 00:52:06,970
cleaner than the web.

1049
00:52:06,970 --> 00:52:09,190
And then adding some
web data and trying

1050
00:52:09,190 --> 00:52:12,700
to add like more
topics was able to help

1051
00:52:12,700 --> 00:52:14,480
us compensate some of the gaps.

1052
00:52:14,480 --> 00:52:17,940
But adding some web always
gives you a performance boost.

1053
00:52:17,940 --> 00:52:21,550
So yes, there is some noise
and some specific patterns

1054
00:52:21,550 --> 00:52:23,890
in web data that
will probably need

1055
00:52:23,890 --> 00:52:25,540
to be included in
the training mix

1056
00:52:25,540 --> 00:52:31,510
to keep a whole coverage of
what's natural distributions

1057
00:52:31,510 --> 00:52:32,680
look like.

1058
00:52:32,680 --> 00:52:36,400
So it sounds like you're
saying a good training

1059
00:52:36,400 --> 00:52:38,410
set would have a
mix, potentially,

1060
00:52:38,410 --> 00:52:40,560
of synthetic and natural data.

1061
00:52:40,560 --> 00:52:41,920
Is that correct?

1062
00:52:41,920 --> 00:52:43,300
Yeah, I think so.

1063
00:52:43,300 --> 00:52:47,200
Some experiments were on show
that that's the case because you

1064
00:52:47,200 --> 00:52:50,530
can try to spend some time to
carefully curate the topics,

1065
00:52:50,530 --> 00:52:53,320
but you'll probably be
missing out on some things.

1066
00:52:53,320 --> 00:52:54,820
And the human
intuition that we have

1067
00:52:54,820 --> 00:52:57,600
is not always what works
for training models.

1068
00:52:57,600 --> 00:53:00,200
It seems that keeping
some filtered web helps.

1069
00:53:00,200 --> 00:53:03,070
And also, if you see the
technical reports, for example,

1070
00:53:03,070 --> 00:53:05,890
in [INAUDIBLE], they insist
a lot on filtering the web

1071
00:53:05,890 --> 00:53:07,760
and including it in
the pre-training.

1072
00:53:07,760 --> 00:53:13,520
And I think that now seems
like maybe the best way to go.

1073
00:53:13,520 --> 00:53:14,460
But that makes sense.

1074
00:53:14,460 --> 00:53:15,650
Great.

1075
00:53:15,650 --> 00:53:19,310
Another question is, is
RLHF type preference data

1076
00:53:19,310 --> 00:53:22,050
more important than
unsupervised pre-training data?

1077
00:53:22,050 --> 00:53:27,470
Should we spend more
resources on RLHF data?

1078
00:53:27,470 --> 00:53:28,860
Yeah, that's a good question.

1079
00:53:28,860 --> 00:53:31,880
So for example, the
unsupervised pre-training

1080
00:53:31,880 --> 00:53:34,340
is mainly to get base
models, but then you

1081
00:53:34,340 --> 00:53:37,080
can't use these base
models as chat assistants.

1082
00:53:37,080 --> 00:53:38,580
You need to do another step.

1083
00:53:38,580 --> 00:53:42,440
So you can either do
RLHF, but nowadays, people

1084
00:53:42,440 --> 00:53:44,030
are just doing
instruction tuning

1085
00:53:44,030 --> 00:53:47,180
without needing to go
through RL where you just

1086
00:53:47,180 --> 00:53:49,850
train the model on pairs of
instructions and solutions,

1087
00:53:49,850 --> 00:53:51,390
and that seems to
work very well.

1088
00:53:51,390 --> 00:53:53,090
And there are now
some methods that

1089
00:53:53,090 --> 00:53:56,660
don't use reinforcement learning
but work as well, for example,

1090
00:53:56,660 --> 00:53:58,340
DPO or ORPO.

1091
00:53:58,340 --> 00:54:00,560
So I think if you want
to chat assistant,

1092
00:54:00,560 --> 00:54:04,280
you definitely need to run
a supervised training on top

1093
00:54:04,280 --> 00:54:07,280
of the unsupervised one,
but it doesn't necessarily

1094
00:54:07,280 --> 00:54:08,480
have to be RLHF.

1095
00:54:08,480 --> 00:54:11,770
There are some other
algorithms now.

1096
00:54:11,770 --> 00:54:12,290
Great.

1097
00:54:12,290 --> 00:54:15,260
And here's a
multimodal question.

1098
00:54:15,260 --> 00:54:17,450
Does multimodal
grounding, for example,

1099
00:54:17,450 --> 00:54:20,360
including images and
videos along with the text,

1100
00:54:20,360 --> 00:54:23,065
reduce the need for so
much text-only data?

1101
00:54:23,065 --> 00:54:26,320

1102
00:54:26,320 --> 00:54:27,800
Yeah what do you mean?

1103
00:54:27,800 --> 00:54:29,200
I'm sorry.

1104
00:54:29,200 --> 00:54:31,990
Well, the question is asking,
does multimodal grounding

1105
00:54:31,990 --> 00:54:33,220
help, basically?

1106
00:54:33,220 --> 00:54:36,400
If you have images and
videos along with the text,

1107
00:54:36,400 --> 00:54:39,880
does this reduce the amount
of text-only data required

1108
00:54:39,880 --> 00:54:42,640
to train models?

1109
00:54:42,640 --> 00:54:45,370
So I can't probably answer
that because I haven't tried,

1110
00:54:45,370 --> 00:54:48,580
but I guess, all for example,
the multimodal models,

1111
00:54:48,580 --> 00:54:51,190
for example, that were
recently released,

1112
00:54:51,190 --> 00:54:54,340
there's always a
significant text portion.

1113
00:54:54,340 --> 00:54:58,780
That seems the case for
most vision language models.

1114
00:54:58,780 --> 00:55:02,900
But yeah, I don't know, really,
about the percentages for each.

1115
00:55:02,900 --> 00:55:03,580
Right, OK.

1116
00:55:03,580 --> 00:55:06,350

1117
00:55:06,350 --> 00:55:07,350
A more general question.

1118
00:55:07,350 --> 00:55:08,975
You probably touched
upon some of this,

1119
00:55:08,975 --> 00:55:10,970
but are there any
major differences

1120
00:55:10,970 --> 00:55:13,850
between training text
versus code models

1121
00:55:13,850 --> 00:55:18,020
other than the training
data being different?

1122
00:55:18,020 --> 00:55:19,290
Yes, that's a good question.

1123
00:55:19,290 --> 00:55:21,170
So the training
data is different.

1124
00:55:21,170 --> 00:55:25,650
Regarding the training itself,
we use a similar architecture.

1125
00:55:25,650 --> 00:55:30,050
For example, StarCoder, it was a
Llama or a Mixtral architecture.

1126
00:55:30,050 --> 00:55:31,550
I think one thing
that you probably

1127
00:55:31,550 --> 00:55:34,400
want is long context
because if you

1128
00:55:34,400 --> 00:55:36,950
want to use these models,
for example, in VS code,

1129
00:55:36,950 --> 00:55:39,020
then you want to add all
the neighboring files

1130
00:55:39,020 --> 00:55:40,050
in the context.

1131
00:55:40,050 --> 00:55:42,570
You should be able to
fit a very large context.

1132
00:55:42,570 --> 00:55:45,830
So we try to do some
long context extension.

1133
00:55:45,830 --> 00:55:49,310
But again, people
also do this for LLMs.

1134
00:55:49,310 --> 00:55:51,450
We also care a lot
about inference.

1135
00:55:51,450 --> 00:55:56,280
So we use first, MQA and then
GQA to have faster inference.

1136
00:55:56,280 --> 00:55:59,780
But these are also techniques
that are implemented for LLMs.

1137
00:55:59,780 --> 00:56:02,340
So I'd say overall,
it's very similar.

1138
00:56:02,340 --> 00:56:04,820
But yeah, maybe we should
prioritize some things

1139
00:56:04,820 --> 00:56:07,640
like having a smaller
model that can

1140
00:56:07,640 --> 00:56:11,840
be used for, for example,
IDE is faster than actually,

1141
00:56:11,840 --> 00:56:15,350
a much larger model that
would need more deployment.

1142
00:56:15,350 --> 00:56:16,265
Yeah.

1143
00:56:16,265 --> 00:56:17,330
All right, great.

1144
00:56:17,330 --> 00:56:19,280
Here's also a general question.

1145
00:56:19,280 --> 00:56:21,780
I guess they're
asking for advice.

1146
00:56:21,780 --> 00:56:24,170
So if you have a very
tiny compute budget,

1147
00:56:24,170 --> 00:56:27,170
for example, a single
GPU, what would you

1148
00:56:27,170 --> 00:56:29,150
recommend prioritizing?

1149
00:56:29,150 --> 00:56:32,790
Let's assume you're
fine tuning a model.

1150
00:56:32,790 --> 00:56:35,340
Yeah, so I think,
for example, now

1151
00:56:35,340 --> 00:56:39,630
there are some great solutions
for on-device deployment

1152
00:56:39,630 --> 00:56:41,970
and fine tuning.

1153
00:56:41,970 --> 00:56:44,340
For example, you can
run quantized models

1154
00:56:44,340 --> 00:56:46,860
with Llama CPP or
other frameworks.

1155
00:56:46,860 --> 00:56:49,800
And with the
techniques like PEFT,

1156
00:56:49,800 --> 00:56:52,530
you don't need to do full model
fine tuning and you should be

1157
00:56:52,530 --> 00:56:56,280
able to run this on one
GPU even in 7B model.

1158
00:56:56,280 --> 00:56:59,370
So I think you should just
find a very well curated data

1159
00:56:59,370 --> 00:57:02,610
sets because quality is more
important than quantity,

1160
00:57:02,610 --> 00:57:06,730
and then use one of these
techniques for easy fine tuning

1161
00:57:06,730 --> 00:57:09,612
and that should work.

1162
00:57:09,612 --> 00:57:10,320
All right, great.

1163
00:57:10,320 --> 00:57:13,000

1164
00:57:13,000 --> 00:57:14,410
Here's a question asking--

1165
00:57:14,410 --> 00:57:16,730
I guess, different
from pre-training,

1166
00:57:16,730 --> 00:57:19,840
but they're saying,
I'm guessing,

1167
00:57:19,840 --> 00:57:21,400
the optimal amount
of training data

1168
00:57:21,400 --> 00:57:27,256
depends heavily on the domain as
well as the task at hand, right?

1169
00:57:27,256 --> 00:57:28,900
Yes, probably.

1170
00:57:28,900 --> 00:57:31,950
Now, we're following the
Chinchilla scaling laws.

1171
00:57:31,950 --> 00:57:35,230
I think they tried to
compare English to code,

1172
00:57:35,230 --> 00:57:38,140
and they found that the
findings still hold.

1173
00:57:38,140 --> 00:57:40,070
But maybe if you go
to another domain,

1174
00:57:40,070 --> 00:57:42,175
I don't know, like medical,
things could change.

1175
00:57:42,175 --> 00:57:44,050
And that's why I mentioned
the DeepSeek paper

1176
00:57:44,050 --> 00:57:46,090
where they mentioned
that it's really

1177
00:57:46,090 --> 00:57:47,468
heavily dependent on data.

1178
00:57:47,468 --> 00:57:49,010
And for them, it
was the same domain.

1179
00:57:49,010 --> 00:57:52,600
They just changed data sets,
going from one generic data set

1180
00:57:52,600 --> 00:57:55,880
to another, well curated one,
and things started changing.

1181
00:57:55,880 --> 00:57:58,580
So I think that's
probably the case.

1182
00:57:58,580 --> 00:58:01,330
But it's underexplored
how these scaling laws

1183
00:58:01,330 --> 00:58:03,230
change depending on domains.

1184
00:58:03,230 --> 00:58:05,110
So it's good to be
aware of that when

1185
00:58:05,110 --> 00:58:08,830
developing models for domains
that are not explored by these--

1186
00:58:08,830 --> 00:58:11,080
Speaking of different
domains, code versus text,

1187
00:58:11,080 --> 00:58:14,230
someone is asking, what are some
of the interesting differences

1188
00:58:14,230 --> 00:58:19,730
between tokenizing for
general purpose text

1189
00:58:19,730 --> 00:58:23,180
versus for code generation?

1190
00:58:23,180 --> 00:58:27,227
Yeah, so when we were
training the tokenizer,

1191
00:58:27,227 --> 00:58:29,060
I think one thing that
was important to keep

1192
00:58:29,060 --> 00:58:33,680
was number splitting, and
we use the standard bpe.

1193
00:58:33,680 --> 00:58:35,110
And we were training it.

1194
00:58:35,110 --> 00:58:38,930
We trained on our data set that
we were using for this training

1195
00:58:38,930 --> 00:58:41,330
data, so our code mixture.

1196
00:58:41,330 --> 00:58:43,490
And we did some
analysis to see if there

1197
00:58:43,490 --> 00:58:47,060
are any outliers,
any tokens that

1198
00:58:47,060 --> 00:58:49,340
were underrepresented
or overrepresented

1199
00:58:49,340 --> 00:58:50,700
as sanity checks.

1200
00:58:50,700 --> 00:58:57,150
But overall, it's very
close to the text training.

1201
00:58:57,150 --> 00:59:00,770
And now, most LLMs have a
significant code portion

1202
00:59:00,770 --> 00:59:04,590
in their tokenizers so they're
also trained on a lot of code.

1203
00:59:04,590 --> 00:59:08,260
And at the end, you can use
either one tokenizer for LLMs

1204
00:59:08,260 --> 00:59:10,880
or for code or the other way.

1205
00:59:10,880 --> 00:59:13,190
Because even on code, you
have a lot of markdown,

1206
00:59:13,190 --> 00:59:15,350
so there's a lot of
English, so you end up

1207
00:59:15,350 --> 00:59:17,660
representing all the
English tokens, for example,

1208
00:59:17,660 --> 00:59:20,622
in your code tokenizer.

1209
00:59:20,622 --> 00:59:21,330
All right, great.

1210
00:59:21,330 --> 00:59:23,372
And here's a question
about fine tuning, I guess,

1211
00:59:23,372 --> 00:59:24,610
compared to pre-training.

1212
00:59:24,610 --> 00:59:30,120
So they're asking, do the same
principles apply for fine tuning

1213
00:59:30,120 --> 00:59:33,580
or would you make a different
or additional recommendation?

1214
00:59:33,580 --> 00:59:36,620

1215
00:59:36,620 --> 00:59:39,170
So yeah, for fine
tuning, I think

1216
00:59:39,170 --> 00:59:42,210
when you're preparing the data,
it's probably a different thing.

1217
00:59:42,210 --> 00:59:45,000
You're not going to train
on all of the stack.

1218
00:59:45,000 --> 00:59:48,260
You probably want to continue
training on specific language.

1219
00:59:48,260 --> 00:59:50,210
So maybe you could
invest more time

1220
00:59:50,210 --> 00:59:52,340
to even heavily filter
because for fine tuning,

1221
00:59:52,340 --> 00:59:55,040
you don't need as much
data as for pre-training.

1222
00:59:55,040 --> 00:59:56,720
For example, for
us, the filtering

1223
00:59:56,720 --> 00:59:58,245
we tried, for
example, stars didn't

1224
00:59:58,245 --> 00:59:59,870
work because they
removed a lot of data

1225
00:59:59,870 --> 01:00:01,970
and we did not have enough
for our pre-training.

1226
01:00:01,970 --> 01:00:04,880
Well, for fine tuning, for
example, for instruction tuning,

1227
01:00:04,880 --> 01:00:07,880
there was the Lima paper where
they instruction tuned only

1228
01:00:07,880 --> 01:00:10,730
on 1,000 instructions and they
had a model that was much better

1229
01:00:10,730 --> 01:00:12,600
than train on
millions of samples.

1230
01:00:12,600 --> 01:00:15,680
So I think that situation is
even much more important when

1231
01:00:15,680 --> 01:00:16,765
it comes to fine tuning.

1232
01:00:16,765 --> 01:00:19,130

1233
01:00:19,130 --> 01:00:19,630
Great.

1234
01:00:19,630 --> 01:00:22,360
One last question, I guess.

1235
01:00:22,360 --> 01:00:24,820
So you might have also
touched upon this briefly,

1236
01:00:24,820 --> 01:00:27,940
but what are some considerations
to make when publishing

1237
01:00:27,940 --> 01:00:32,050
very large data sets, and more
nuanced or less known things

1238
01:00:32,050 --> 01:00:35,190
to be aware of?

1239
01:00:35,190 --> 01:00:40,870
Yeah, so maybe on
the technical side,

1240
01:00:40,870 --> 01:00:44,640
releasing tools also for
filtering and documentation.

1241
01:00:44,640 --> 01:00:46,620
That's what we tried
to do with the stack.

1242
01:00:46,620 --> 01:00:49,870
And maybe more on
the governance side,

1243
01:00:49,870 --> 01:00:53,130
be aware of where the
licenses are respected, where

1244
01:00:53,130 --> 01:00:54,410
the copyright is respected.

1245
01:00:54,410 --> 01:00:56,970
Do you have an opt out
tool for your data sets?

1246
01:00:56,970 --> 01:00:59,400
And maybe try to
release it on the hub

1247
01:00:59,400 --> 01:01:01,750
to make it easily
accessible for people.

1248
01:01:01,750 --> 01:01:04,270
If there are some concerns,
you could try to add a gate.

1249
01:01:04,270 --> 01:01:06,210
For example, for us,
we release the data set

1250
01:01:06,210 --> 01:01:09,900
that we used for PII detection,
but we add some gating mechanism

1251
01:01:09,900 --> 01:01:11,950
because it was
sensitive information.

1252
01:01:11,950 --> 01:01:15,060
So it's good to think of
this things in advance

1253
01:01:15,060 --> 01:01:17,490
before releasing the data sets.

1254
01:01:17,490 --> 01:01:19,460
But yeah, in general,
these are my advice.

1255
01:01:19,460 --> 01:01:22,470

1256
01:01:22,470 --> 01:01:24,060
Great.

1257
01:01:24,060 --> 01:01:27,420
Do we have any
in-person questions?

1258
01:01:27,420 --> 01:01:30,860
If not, then we can
probably conclude.

1259
